     * [`DspiError::Denied`] when the Link hub refuses its lock (nothing is
     * sent); the transport's typed error when a transfer fails (the device
     * then applies nothing).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun applyAllParams()

    /**
     * Read the meter block (`REQ_GET_STATUS` sub-query 9): the peak of each
     * live wire channel (0..1), both cores' loads (%), the sticky clip
     * flags (bit per wire channel) and the live input count.
     *
     * `peaks` always has `MAX_CHANNELS` (17) entries; those past
     * `num_channels` are 0. `active_input_channels` is 0 from firmware that
     * does not append it. Works on a read-only connection too.
     *
     * Over DSPi Link this is the latest frame of a `poll.subscribe` (no
     * round trip, and it never delays a command): subscribed on the first
     * call, unsubscribed about two seconds after the last, so stop calling
     * when no meter is on screen. Until the first frame, or if the feed
     * stalls, the block reads all zero with `stale` set. A hub that refuses the
     * poll is read directly at a low rate; a repeat of the last such read is
     * `stale` too.
     *
     * WP P0M: `peak_hold`, `peak_max` and `clip_hold` are the core's meter
     * ballistics over these reads (`set_meter_ballistics`); a `stale` read
     * leaves them as they were. When the clip hold ends the core clears the
     * device's sticky flags itself (`REQ_CLEAR_CLIPS`, as `clear_clips`), so
     * a client keeps no clip timer. A channel still clipping lights again on
     * the next read; where the clear is refused (a viewer on a hub) the hold
     * follows the device's sticky flags. New clips go to the event log.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device open;
     * [`DspiError::Transfer`] when the read fails or the reply is shorter
     * than `num_channels * 2 + 6` bytes.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchStatus(): SystemStatus

    /**
     * Copy of the cached state (the C ABI returns a borrowed pointer).
     * Defaults while disconnected. Never fails. Under a named crossfeed
     * preset, `crossfeed_freq` and `crossfeed_feed` are that preset's values
     * (see `set_crossfeed_preset`).
     */
    fun getState(): DspState

    /**
     * Longest delay the firmware's delay line holds, ms: 42 on RP2350
     * (`platform_id` 1, 2048 samples at 48 kHz), 21 otherwise (RP2040,
     * 1024 samples). Read from the mirror; never fails.
     */
    fun maxDelayMs(): Float

    /**
     * Clear the device's sticky clip flags (`REQ_CLEAR_CLIPS`, which reads
     * the flags back and clears them; the value is not used).
     *
     * Meter housekeeping, not a parameter: it touches no setting and
     * nothing in the bulk image, so it is allowed on a read-only
     * connection too. This is the one named exception (PLAN §1.2) to
     * "state-changing opcodes only on a `Writable`": it goes out on a plain
     * `Connection`.
     *
     * -- WP ROMODEF -- Not in the client read-only mode, which writes
     * nothing, the device's counters included: [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * nothing sent, the clip hold left as it is.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device open; the
     * transport's typed error when the transfer fails.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun clearClips()

    /**
     * Read the whole control-surface configuration from the device:
     * capabilities, bindings and names, IR commands, groups, macros, the
     * display and the aux outputs (about seventy round trips).
     *
     * Firmware without control surfaces (or a device shown without a
     * session, PLAN §1.5) reads as unsupported and is `Ok`. Reads only:
     * allowed on a read-only connection.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] without a device; a transfer
     * failure (lost device, timeout) as its typed error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun csFetch()

    /**
     * The control-surface configuration as JSON, in the old core's shape
     * (`{supported, capsVersion, maxBindings, types, nouns, maxIr,
     * maxGroups, maxMacros, maxSteps, status, bindings, names, ir, groups,
     * macros, ext, display, auxState, auxLevel, learn}`), from the last
     * [`DspiCore::cs_fetch`] and the operations since. `{"supported":
     * false}` before the first fetch of the open device, without a device,
     * or on firmware without control surfaces. Never fails.
     */
    fun csSnapshot(): String

    /**
     * One control-surface operation, JSON `{op, index, ...}` (the old
     * core's ops, which the Qt `CsController` sends): `refresh`,
     * `refreshStatus`, `binding`, `name`, `ir`, `group`, `macro`,
     * `displayConfig`, `displayPage`, `displayStatus`, `save`, `revert`,
     * `learnArm`, `learnCancel`, `learnRead`, `macroFire`, `macroCancel`,
     * `auxState`, `auxLevel`, `auxRead`. Each write re-reads what it
     * changed; the first operation on a device not yet fetched fetches it.
     *
     * Returns JSON `{ok, status}` (the device's status code; `0x16` = still
     * pending) plus op-specific fields, or `{ok: false, error}` for a
     * malformed operation (bad JSON, a number out of its field's range, an
     * index the device does not have, an unknown op), a write on a
     * read-only connection (nothing is sent), firmware without control
     * surfaces, no device, or a transfer failure. `save` writes flash: the
     * bridge gates it (DangerousOps).
     *
     * `learnArm` holds the device lock for the 15 s learn window (WP CG24),
     * so another DSPi Link session gets `locked` meanwhile (and a refused
     * lock is `{ok: false}` with nothing sent). A `learnRead` whose `state`
     * is no longer ARMED, `learnCancel`, the window's end or the
     * connection's close releases it (a failed `learnRead` does not);
     * [`DspiCore::device_lock_release`] does not. Inside a lock taken with
     * [`DspiCore::device_lock_acquire`] a learn takes no lock of its own and
     * lasts only as long as that one.
     */
    fun csApply(op: String): String

    /**
     * GPIOs held by live control-surface bindings, as (pin, slot): a
     * binding reserves its pins only while its active bit is set. From the
     * last read; empty before the first [`DspiCore::cs_fetch`] of the open
     * device.
     */
    fun csPinUses(): List<PinUse>

    /**
     * On the demo device only: hold it in a running revert, or let it finish.
     * While held it gives the refusals a real device has no steady way to show:
     * a write settles busy, and the save, the revert, a macro fire and a learn
     * are refused. As [`core_vreg_demo_event`](Self::core_vreg_demo_event), it
     * answers [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on any other device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun csDemoBusy(on: Boolean)

    /**
     * What a page shows for one record, from the last [`DspiCore::cs_fetch`]:
     * `{ok, title, summary, rows}`. `kind` is `binding`, `ir`, `step`, `page`,
     * `display` or `group`; `slot` is the record's index; `draft` is the record as
     * in [`DspiCore::cs_snapshot`].
     *
     * Each row has `id`, `title`, `detail` and `kind`. A `pick` has `value`, `text`
     * and `options` of `{value, text, section?, owner?, disabled?}`; a `number`
     * has `value`, `min`, `max`, `step`, `unit` and `decimals`, in natural units; a
     * `toggle` has `on` and `enabled`; a `note` has `warning`, and its words in
     * `detail`. `dspi_session::cs_rows` lists the ids.
     *
     * No device I/O. `{ok: false, error}` on a bad draft, an index the device does
     * not have, no device, or before the first fetch. Never fails otherwise.
     */
    fun csDescribe(kind: String, slot: Long, draft: String): String

    /**
     * The record after one row's value changed: `{ok, draft}`. `change` is
     * `{id, value}`: the row's id, and an option's value, a number in natural units,
     * or a boolean. The edit resets what the change leaves meaningless, so the
     * record stays one the firmware takes.
     *
     * No device I/O: nothing changes on the device until the record goes to
     * [`DspiCore::cs_apply`]. `{ok: false, error}` when the row or the value is not
     * one [`DspiCore::cs_describe`] offers for this record, or as `cs_describe`.
     */
    fun csEdit(kind: String, slot: Long, draft: String, change: String): String

    /**
     * A fresh binding of type `component` in `slot`, on free pins: `{ok, draft}`.
     * No device I/O. `{ok: false, error}` as [`DspiCore::cs_describe`].
     */
    fun csNewBinding(slot: Long, component: Int): String

    /**
     * A fresh record of `kind`, `ir`, `step` or `page`: `{ok, draft}`. A step and a
     * page are ready to apply; a remote button needs its code from a learn first.
     * No device I/O. `{ok: false, error}` as [`DspiCore::cs_describe`].
     */
    fun csNewRecord(kind: String): String

    /**
     * A control-surface or pin-configuration status code as a sentence; empty for
     * success. Needs no device.
     */
    fun csStatusText(code: Int): String

    /**
     * On the demo device only: the remote button a learn is waiting for was
     * pressed. The learn ends with this protocol and code and notifies, as on a
     * real receiver. False when nothing was armed. As
     * [`cs_demo_busy`](Self::cs_demo_busy), it answers [`DspiError::Unsupported`]
     * on any other device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun csDemoLearn(protocol: Int, code: Long): Boolean

    /**
     * Adopt a USB device the platform already opened: Android's
     * `UsbDeviceConnection.getFileDescriptor()`. The core `dup(2)`s the fd
     * and owns the duplicate, so the app keeps (and later closes) its own;
     * it claims the vendor interface 2 only, never the audio interfaces.
     * Replaces any open device, then runs the connect sequence; on failure
     * nothing is connected (the previous device is closed either way).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a negative or unusable
     * `fd`; [`DspiError::Disconnected`] if it is not a DSPi; transfer
     * errors from the connect sequence. Linux and Android only, with the
     * `usb` feature; [`DspiError::Unsupported`] elsewhere.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun openFd(fd: Int, serial: String, vid: Int)

    /**
     * Close the device opened by [`DspiCore::open_fd`] (the same as
     * [`DspiCore::disconnect`]); afterwards the app may close its
     * `UsbDeviceConnection`. Not called `close` because UniFFI's Kotlin
     * objects already have `close()` (free the object).
     */
    fun closeFd()

    /**
     * Enumerate DSPis on USB (nusb): VID `2E8B` or the legacy VID, PID
     * `FEAA`, with a readable serial, in serial order. Updates
     * [`DspiCore::inaccessible_devices`]. Empty on Android, where apps
     * cannot enumerate (use `open_fd`), and in a build without the `usb`
     * feature (iOS). Never fails: an enumeration error gives an empty list.
     */
    fun scanDevices(): List<DeviceInfo>

    /**
     * Highest preamp (dB) the connected chip applies correctly: +18 on an
     * RP2040 (its Q28 multiplier overflows above), +24 on an RP2350. +18
     * while nothing is connected (the mirror's default platform).
     */
    fun preampMaxDb(): Float

    /**
     * DSPis the last scan saw but could not open: no permission (Linux udev
     * rule), no WinUSB binding, or held by another program.
     */
    fun inaccessibleDevices(): Long

    /**
     * Open the USB DSPi with this serial and run the connect sequence,
     * replacing any open device. The serial is remembered for reconnection
     * (WP C1b) even if the open fails, so a device that arrives later is
     * picked up.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] if no DSPi with that serial is on
     * the bus; transfer errors (no permission, busy) from opening it or from
     * the connect sequence. [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on Android (use
     * [`DspiCore::open_fd`]) and in a build without the `usb` feature (iOS).
     * With a [`crate::DeviceBroker`] set, the broker opens it instead, on any
     * platform.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun selectDevice(serial: String)

    /**
     * Close the open device, whatever its transport, and reset the mirror
     * to its defaults. The selected serial is kept, so a replugged device
     * reconnects (WP C1b). Blocks until the notification reader has stopped
     * (at most about a quarter of a second).
     */
    fun disconnect()

    /**
     * Whether a device is open: `conn.is_some()`. It stays true after an
     * unplug until the connection is closed (by C1b's hotplug poll, C7's
     * notification handling, or [`DspiCore::disconnect`]); a call on it
     * then fails with [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    fun isConnected(): Boolean

    /**
     * The `wire_compat::feat` bits the connected firmware's wire format carries:
     * every known bit on V32 and newer, none with no device or below V16.
     */
    fun wireFeatures(): Long

    /**
     * Open the built-in demo device (`dspi_transport::FakeDevice::demo`):
     * a simulated RP2350 that answers reads and writes and notifies.
     * Replaces any open device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun openDemo()

    /**
     * Whether the connect probe found feature `name` on the open device
     * (`dspi_session::probe`'s names: `spdif_multi_input`, `adat_input`,
     * `i2s_slave_clock`, ...), so a client hides what the firmware lacks
     * without comparing versions. `i2s_clock_pin_mode`, which has no probe,
     * answers whether the parameter image carried the clock-pin mode. False
     * with no device, before the probe, and for a name the probe does not
     * know. -- WP CG-SWS-a -- The names with no probe (`first_order_pass`,
     * `subharmonic_extended`, `stereo_upmixer`, ...) are answered from the
     * wire format, firmware version and platform the connect read;
     * [`known_feature_names`] lists every name.
     */
    fun featurePresent(name: String): Boolean

    /**
     * [`crate::bulk_image_extras`] of the mirror's parameter image: the
     * bytes `DspState` has no field for. All false with no image.
     */
    fun imageExtras(): BulkImageExtras

    /**
     * Write a diagnostics bundle to `path`: one zip with versions, the open
     * device's dump, stats and monitor lines, every event log, the hub
     * store at `options.hub_store_dir` with its tokens and secrets removed,
     * the OS and USB facts, and `options.files`. Nothing is sent anywhere.
     * Returns the names of the files in the zip.
     *
     * Reads the device (a Stats window's reads) and files: not on the UI
     * thread. The zip is written beside `path` first and renamed over it,
     * so a failure leaves no half file.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Io`] when the file cannot be written;
     * [`DspiError::Unsupported`] where there is no file system (the web,
     * which saves [`DspiCore::diagnostics_zip`]'s bytes itself). What the
     * bundle could not read is in its `notes.txt`, never an error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun diagnosticsSave(path: String, options: DiagOptions): List<String>

    /**
     * A file name for a bundle saved now: `dspi-diagnostics-` and the UTC
     * date and time, `.zip`. For a Save dialog's suggestion. Never fails.
     */
    fun diagnosticsFileName(): String

    /**
     * The meter ballistics' timing (`SystemStatus.peak_hold`, `clip_hold`).
     * Defaults: hold 1000 ms, then a fall of 0.5 full scale per second; clip
     * hold 10 000 ms after the last new clip. Never fails.
     */
    fun meterBallistics(): MeterBallistics

    /**
     * Change the meter ballistics' timing for every later `fetch_status`.
     * What is held stays. A `decay_per_s` below 0 or not finite reads as 0
     * (a held peak then stays until a higher one); a `clip_hold_ms` of 0
     * holds clips until `clear_clips`. Never fails.
     */
    fun setMeterBallistics(config: MeterBallistics)

    /**
     * Start every channel's `SystemStatus.peak_max` again from the next
     * read. A new connection does this too. Never fails.
     */
    fun resetMeterMax()

    /**
     * Keep the event log in files under `dir`, the client's own data
     * directory, created if missing: one JSON-lines file per device serial,
     * rotated, at most about 1 MiB per device. An empty `dir` keeps it in
     * memory only (the default; the last 1000 records). Call it at start-up,
     * before connecting; the open device's log carries on in the new
     * place. Blocking file I/O: not on the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Io`] when the directory cannot be created;
     * [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on the web, which keeps it in memory.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun eventLogSetDir(dir: String)

    /**
     * `serial`'s event log with ids above `after_id`, oldest first, at
     * most `limit` (capped at [`EVENT_READ_MAX`]): with `after_id` 0 the
     * newest `limit`, otherwise the next `limit` after it, so poll with the
     * batch's `last_id`. An empty `serial` is the open device. Lines in the
     * file that are not records are skipped and counted. Reads files: not on
     * the UI thread; no other call waits for it. Never fails; an unknown
     * serial reads empty.
     */
    fun eventLogRead(serial: String, afterId: Long, limit: Long): EventBatch

    /**
     * `serial`'s whole event log as JSON lines, oldest first, in the file
     * format (`dspi_session::events`): for a "Save events…" or a diagnostics
     * bundle. An empty `serial` is the open device. Never fails; an unknown
     * serial exports "".
     */
    fun eventLogExport(serial: String): String

    /**
     * The serials with an event log, sorted. Never fails.
     */
    fun eventLogDevices(): List<String>

    /**
     * `EXT_CROSSFEED_PAIR_MAP` (Bofr5555/DSPi fork, the K340 build): the
     * outputs of every crossfeed pair at once, 4 bytes
     * (`DspState.crossfeed_pair_map` encoding).
     *
     * The map is checked first: `InvalidArgument`, with no traffic, for a
     * map the firmware would refuse ([`crate::crossfeed_pair_map_valid`] for
     * the platform's pair count). It is then sent normalized and read back,
     * because the firmware ACKs an invalid map and ignores it; the mirror
     * (`crossfeed_pair_map` and `bulk_raw`) takes what the device reports.
     *
     * Errors: `Disconnected`; `ReadOnly` on a read-only connection;
     * `Unsupported` when the device has no usable pair map (not listed, or
     * its probe read failed); `InvalidArgument` as above, or when the
     * read-back shows the device kept another map; `Refused { status:
     * EXT_WRITE_REFUSED }` when the device stalls the write; `Denied`,
     * `Locked` or `Transfer` from the link.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setCrossfeedPairMap(map: List<Int>)

    /**
     * Read the live pair map (4 bytes) into the mirror: `crossfeed_pair_map`
     * and `bulk_raw`, which carries it in the crossfeed section.
     *
     * `Unsupported`, with no traffic, when the device has no usable pair
     * map (stock firmware stalls `EXT_LIST`; a DSPi Link hub refuses a
     * viewer), or when the read stalls or is refused: the mirror then keeps
     * the value decoded from the bulk image (all zeros, the default
     * pairing, on stock firmware). `Disconnected` without a device;
     * `Transfer` for a short or failed read.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchCrossfeedPairMap(): List<Int>

    /**
     * What the extension channel offers on the connected device, as probed
     * on connect; no traffic. Empty on stock firmware. `has_pair_map` and
     * `has_core_vreg` are true only when the sub-command was listed *and*
     * its probe read succeeded. `Disconnected` without a device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun extStatus(): ExtStatus

    /**
     * Read `EXT_CORE_VREG` (16 bytes) and refresh the connection's copy.
     *
     * `Unsupported`, with no traffic, when the device has no usable
     * core-voltage extension, and when the read stalls or a DSPi Link hub
     * refuses it; `Disconnected` without a device; `Transfer` for a short
     * or failed read.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchCoreVreg(): CoreVregInfo

    /**
     * `EXT_CORE_VREG` write: `op` 0 set (value = VREG code, 0 = build
     * default), 1 trial, 2 end trial, 3 auto (value 0/1), 4 clear
     * (`dspi_proto::ext::VregOp`).
     *
     * Checked before anything is sent (`InvalidArgument`, no traffic): an
     * unknown op; a SET below the build default (the floor,
     * `CoreVregInfo::default_vreg`) or above 0x0F (1.30 V); a TRIAL outside
     * 0x0C..=0x0F (1.15-1.30 V); an AUTO value other than 0 or 1. The value
     * of TRIAL_END and CLEAR is ignored (sent as 0). Read the status back
     * with [`DspiCore::fetch_core_vreg`].
     *
     * WP CG14: a SET or TRIAL reads the unit's status first and goes through
     * `dspi_session::overvolt`: a raise above 1.20 V from what it reports now
     * needs `word`, exactly `OVERVOLT`, or nothing is written. This is the
     * one path to 1.30 V; the GUIs' flow stops at 1.25 V (`core_vreg_cal_*`).
     *
     * -- WP P1TZF -- A SET stores the voltage in flash: it takes a
     * confirmation token ([`crate::ConfirmOp::VregSave`], as
     * [`DspiCore::core_vreg_manual_set_confirmed`]), checked first, before
     * anything is sent. The other operations (a trial, its end, AUTO, CLEAR)
     * take none, as the plan says; a token given with one is still checked
     * and spent. Mint one per try: a refusal for the word after the token
     * was accepted has spent it.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::ConfirmationRequired`] for a SET without a good
     * token; `OvervoltWordRequired` as above; `Disconnected`; `ReadOnly` on
     * a read-only connection;
     * `Unsupported` when the device has no usable core-voltage extension;
     * `Refused { status: EXT_WRITE_REFUSED }` when the device stalls the
     * request anyway; `Denied`, `Locked` or `Transfer` from the link.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun coreVregOpConfirmed(op: Int, value: Int, word: String?, token: String?)

    /**
     * `EXT_IDENTIFY`: blink the device's LED for `seconds` (0 stops it; over
     * 120 the firmware shortens to 120) and, with `tone_output`, play its
     * quiet identify tone ([`IDENTIFY_TONE_DBFS`], 400 ms) on that output.
     * Leave `tone_output` empty unless the user asked for sound.
     *
     * Errors, with no traffic: `Disconnected`; `Unsupported` when the device
     * did not list it; `InvalidArgument` for a tone output past the
     * device's outputs; `ReadOnly` on a read-only connection. From the
     * device: `Refused { status: EXT_WRITE_REFUSED }` when it stalls (a tone
     * while its signal generator runs); `Denied`, `Locked` or `Transfer`
     * from the link.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun identify(seconds: Int, toneOutput: Int?)

    /**
     * Read `EXT_IDENTIFY`: the blink time left and the board description.
     *
     * `Unsupported`, with no traffic, when the device did not list it, and
     * when the read stalls or a DSPi Link hub refuses it; `Disconnected`
     * without a device; `Transfer` for a short or failed read.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchIdentify(): IdentifyInfo

    /**
     * Read `EXT_RESET_INFO`: why the device last restarted, its uptime and
     * the run before. Also refreshes the connection's copy from the probe.
     *
     * `Unsupported`, with no traffic, when the device did not list it, and
     * when the read stalls or a DSPi Link hub refuses it; `Disconnected`
     * without a device; `Transfer` for a short or failed read.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchResetInfo(): DeviceResetInfo

    /**
     * The reset readout as the connect probe found it, without traffic:
     * `None` when the device has no `EXT_RESET_INFO` or the read failed.
     * Its uptime is the one at connect. `Disconnected` without a device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun resetInfoAtConnect(): DeviceResetInfo?

    /**
     * Whether the device sends clip events (`EXT_NOTIFY_FEATURES` bit 1):
     * then a clip shows without polling the meters fast. No traffic;
     * `Disconnected` without a device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hasClipEvents(): Boolean

    /**
     * The device's configuration as a `.dspipreset` document (JSON, schema
     * 1), from the mirror: no transfer. `name` and `app_version` go into
     * `meta`; `links` is the app's input-pair link mask (bit p: pair p),
     * written as `global.inputPairLinked`.
     *
     * The document holds what the device's wire format carries: a key or
     * block that firmware lacks is left out, `crossfeed.pairMap` is written
     * only when the device reports a map over the extension channel, and
     * the upmixer only on an RP2350. A read-only device (another wire
     * format) exports what its image showed.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device;
     * [`DspiError::Transfer`] before its settings were read;
     * [`DspiError::Unsupported`] for firmware whose image this build cannot
     * map.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun configExport(name: String, appVersion: String, links: Int): String

    /**
     * Apply a `.dspipreset` document to the device as one bulk write, under
     * the device lock, with a confirmation token. `options`:
     * [`IMPORT_VOLUMES`] also applies the master and user volume,
     * [`IMPORT_HARDWARE`] the hardware I/O and the limiters. The crossfeed pair map goes over the extension channel when
     * the device has one.
     *
     * Every setting the document holds no readable value for keeps the
     * device's value: a missing key or block, a value of the wrong type, an
     * `eq` or `crossover` with no entries. A bank with entries replaces the
     * channel's, the rest going flat. Delays past the device's delay line
     * are shortened to it. Channels the device lacks are named, not moved.
     *
     * Returns JSON. On success `{ok: true, error: "", lines, clean, links,
     * hardwareWritten, leftAlone, outOfRange}`: `lines` is the report,
     * `clean` false when anything was missing, skipped, left alone,
     * shortened or out of range, `leftAlone` the keys left alone,
     * `outOfRange` the numbers past what this device takes, each
     * `{path, value, applied}` (registry path with indices, the document's
     * value, the nearest end written instead; WP P0U's rows), `links` the input-pair link mask to
     * restore (null when the document has none), and `hardwareWritten`
     * whether I/O was changed while the device stores its output
     * configuration apart from presets (save that to keep it). Otherwise
     * `{ok: false, error}`: the text is not a document this build reads, or
     * the device could not be written (disconnected, read-only firmware,
     * another console's lock, a failed transfer); the mirror then stays as
     * it was.
     *
     * The EQ is narrowed to the wire channels in `eq_channels` (bit k:
     * channel k; `u32::MAX` every one). With [`IMPORT_BY_BLOCK`] in
     * `options` each `IMPORT_*` block bit says whether its block is applied (EQ, crossover, routing and matrix,
     * outputs, limiters, effects, volume levels, hardware I/O, names), and
     * a block left out keeps the device's values; without it the old two
     * bits keep their meaning. The reply also carries `blocks`: the block
     * bits applied.
     *
     * With [`IMPORT_BY_BLOCK`] and no block bit nothing is chosen: the
     * reply is a refusal (`noticeKey` `config.nothing_selected`) and nothing
     * is written, journalled or logged; the undo history stays as it was.
     *
     * The token ([`crate::confirm`], [`crate::ConfirmOp::ConfigImport`];
     * required since WP P1TZ): without a good one the reply is a refusal
     * (`noticeKey` `confirmation_required`) and nothing is written. A text
     * that is not a document, or an empty selection, is refused first and
     * leaves the token unspent. There is no form without a token.
     */
    fun configImportConfirmed(text: String, options: Long, eqChannels: Long, token: String?): String

    /**
     * What importing `text` with `options` and `eq_channels` (as
     * [`DspiCore::config_import_confirmed`] takes them) would change on the
     * device, without writing anything: the preview shown before an import
     * (PRO1 W5). No I/O.
     *
     * Returns [`config_diff`]'s JSON, the device's values as `before`.
     * `{ok: false, error}` for a text that is not a document this build
     * reads, or with no device whose settings have been read.
     */
    fun configPreview(text: String, options: Long, eqChannels: Long): String

    /**
     * Every channel's bands as a filter file (format 2), from the mirror:
     * the first `inputs` inputs with their preamp and EQ bands, then every
     * output with its EQ and crossover bands and its enable state.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::config_export`], when the mirror holds no
     * device's state.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun filtersExport(inputs: Int): String

    /**
     * What a filter file holds, for the channel picker, as JSON; never
     * fails. A Console file (macOS, Windows or this one) gives
     * `{kind: "native", channels, newer}`: each channel it has a section
     * for on this device, ticked, and whether a newer format wrote it. A
     * REW or AutoEQ list gives `{kind: "rew", channels, filters, preamp}`:
     * the first `inputs` inputs (ticked) and every output (not ticked), the
     * number of filters and the preamp (null without one). A channel is
     * `{wire, label, bands, checked}`. An empty `channels` means nothing in
     * the file applies; a file over [`MAX_FILE_BYTES`] gives `kind`
     * "error" with an `error`.
     */
    fun filtersInspect(text: String, inputs: Int): String

    /**
     * Apply a filter file to the wire channels in `wires` (sections for
     * other channels are ignored), through the band, preamp and output
     * enable setters, under the device lock. A section's EQ or crossover
     * lines replace that bank, the rest going flat; a section without lines
     * for a bank leaves it alone, and the report says so. A REW list goes to
     * every chosen channel, its preamp to the inputs only.
     *
     * Returns JSON `{ok, error, lines}`: `ok` false with `error` when the
     * file is over [`MAX_FILE_BYTES`], nothing in it applies to the chosen
     * channels, or the device could not be written (disconnected, read-only
     * firmware, a failed transfer; channels written before a failure stay
     * written).
     */
    fun filtersImport(text: String, wires: List<Int>): String

    /**
     * Whether the open device runs firmware `version`: the leading
     * `major.minor.patch[-betaN]` of a UF2's `Uf2Info::firmware_version`
     * (`1.1.6-beta3-16-g557bce7` compares as 1.1.6 beta 3), against the
     * mirror's `fw_*` from `GET_PLATFORM`. The last step of a firmware
     * update, after the device re-enumerated and was opened again. False
     * for a string that does not start with a version; an early 1.1.6 beta
     * that does not report its number matches betas 1 and 2.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device open.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun firmwareVersionMatches(version: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Write `image` to the bootloader drive behind the firmware guard
     * ([`crate::firmware_guard_check`] for `board` and `typed_confirmation`),
     * with a confirmation token ([`crate::confirm`],
     * [`crate::ConfirmOp::FirmwareFlash`]): the one to use wherever the core
     * does the writing. Mint the token once the bootloader drive is there
     * (the board has left, so a token minted while it was still connected no
     * longer holds); it works with no device open, and a board already in
     * BOOTSEL has none. Without a good token, or with one for another
     * operation, [`DspiError::ConfirmationRequired`] and nothing reaches the
     * drive. It is the only drive write the bindings have (WP P1TZF): a
     * caller that knows the board passes it, and one in BOOTSEL passes
     * [`crate::firmware_guard_board_in_bootloader`] with the typed phrase
     * the verdict asks for.
     *
     * Before any byte goes out: the image is fully validated, the drive's
     * `INFO_UF2.TXT` is read again and must still name the board `drive`
     * describes, the image's family must be one that board boots, and the
     * drive must have room for the image. The board reboots into the new
     * firmware as it takes the last block, so `Ok` means only "the write
     * ended, or the drive went away during it": the caller confirms success
     * by waiting for the DSPi to re-enumerate and report the image's version
     * (`Uf2Info::firmware_version`); a timeout there is the failure.
     *
     * Errors: that; [`DspiError::FirmwareGuardBlocked`] with the guard's
     * message when the image needs confirmation; then the write's.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun flashConfirmed(image: List<Int>, drive: BootloaderDrive, board: FirmwareGuardBoard, typedConfirmation: String, token: String?)

    /**
     * What the open board needs, read from the device now. Call before
     * `enter_bootloader`, and keep the record for [`firmware_guard_check`].
     *
     * Never fails: no device, or any answer that cannot be believed, gives
     * an unknown board, which the check then blocks. Blocks for up to three
     * control transfers.
     */
    fun firmwareGuardBoard(): FirmwareGuardBoard

    /**
     * Import `text` (a `.dspipreset` document) with a way back: capture a
     * snapshot, apply the blocks `options` and `eq_channels` choose (as
     * [`DspiCore::config_import_confirmed`] takes them), then wait
     * `timeout_s` seconds (0: 30, at most 590) for
     * [`DspiCore::apply_confirm`]. A timeout, [`DspiCore::apply_revert`] or a
     * failed import puts the snapshot back; over DSPi Link the hub guards it
     * too. No confirmation token: a guarded apply has its own way back.
     *
     * Returns the state, [`GuardPhase::Waiting`] with the changes.
     *
     * Errors, with nothing applied: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a text
     * that is not a document, an empty selection, or while another guarded
     * apply waits; [`DspiError::Disconnected`], [`DspiError::ReadOnly`];
     * [`DspiError::Link`] for the hub's refusal (`busy`, `locked`,
     * `denied`, `no_device`). The import's own errors, after the snapshot
     * was put back (see [`DspiCore::apply_status`]).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun applyGuardedImport(text: String, options: Long, eqChannels: Long, timeoutS: Long): GuardedApply

    /**
     * Recall snapshot `id` from the store with a way back, as
     * [`DspiCore::apply_guarded_import`] does for a document. Errors as
     * there, and [`DspiCore::snapshot_recall`]'s.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun applyGuardedSnapshot(id: String, timeoutS: Long): GuardedApply

    /**
     * Recall a snapshot document's text with a way back (the web's, which
     * keeps snapshots as files the page holds). Only a snapshot: any other
     * document is refused with [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`], nothing sent.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun applyGuardedSnapshotDocument(text: String, timeoutS: Long): GuardedApply

    /**
     * Begin a guard around a flow the client runs itself (output enables, a
     * control-surface edit, several hardware settings): capture a snapshot
     * now, then the client writes as usual and calls
     * [`DspiCore::apply_confirm`] or [`DspiCore::apply_revert`]; the timeout
     * and a lost connection work as for an import. `label` names the flow
     * in the status. Errors as [`DspiCore::apply_guarded_import`]'s before
     * anything is applied.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun applyGuardBegin(label: String, timeoutS: Long): GuardedApply

    /**
     * Keep the change. Returns the state: [`GuardPhase::Kept`]; or, past
     * the timeout, the revert's end; or [`GuardPhase::Lost`] when the Keep
     * could not reach the hub (it is not sent again: the hub reverts).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when nothing is waiting.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun applyConfirm(): GuardedApply

    /**
     * Put the settings from before back now. Returns the state:
     * [`GuardPhase::Reverted`], [`GuardPhase::RevertFailed`], or
     * [`GuardPhase::Lost`] when the connection went. After a lost USB
     * connection, it recalls the captured snapshot once the same device is
     * open again (the user's word, never queued).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when nothing is waiting and no
     * lost apply can be reverted on the open device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun applyRevert(): GuardedApply

    /**
     * Drive the guard: revert at the timeout, notice a lost connection, ask
     * the hub how a lost one ended once the connection is back. Call it
     * from the client's poll, off the UI thread (it may write). Returns the
     * state; at once, unchanged, while another guarded call runs.
     */
    fun applyPoll(): GuardedApply

    /**
     * The guard's state, for the countdown bar: no I/O, never blocks on a
     * guarded call.
     */
    fun applyStatus(): GuardedApply

    /**
     * Whether an import or a recall is guarded by default here (owner,
     * OD-P1g): over DSPi Link to a hub that offers the guard, yes; on USB
     * it is the user's choice. An A/B toggle never is.
     */
    fun applyGuardDefault(): Boolean

    /**
     * Move physical output `slot` (0..`num_pin_outputs`) to GPIO `pin`;
     * `PIN_RESET_TO_DEFAULT` (0xFF) restores the platform default, which is
     * then read back. The firmware applies the move later through a muted
     * pipeline reset; it is live only until a preset or output-config save.
     * If the reset is accepted but its read-back fails, the error is
     * returned and the mirror keeps the old pin: re-fetch it with
     * [`DspiCore::fetch_output_pin`].
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `PIN_CONFIG_INVALID_OUTPUT` (no
     * such slot), `INVALID_PIN`, `PIN_IN_USE`, or `OUTPUT_ACTIVE` (the PDM
     * slot while PDM runs), the mirror unchanged; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`], [`DspiError::Locked`] (Link, reset only)
     * or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setOutputPin(slot: Int, pin: Int)

    /**
     * Read the GPIO of physical output `slot` into `DspState.output_pins`
     * and return it.
     *
     * Errors: a transfer error (the device stalls on a slot it does not
     * have); [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchOutputPin(slot: Int): Int

    /**
     * Where the physical I/O configuration is stored: 0 = independently of
     * the presets (saved with [`DspiCore::save_output_config_confirmed`]), 1 = with
     * each preset. A device-global flash write the firmware runs later.
     * Sets `DspState.output_config_mode`.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a mode above 1 (the
     * firmware would silently take it as 0); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setOutputConfigMode(mode: Int)

    /**
     * FLASH WRITE: persist the live physical I/O configuration (the
     * independent mode's explicit save). The firmware writes it later in
     * its main loop. Runs under the DSPi Link device lock: the deferred
     * flash write blacks out the device's control path for tens of
     * milliseconds, so other consoles must not interleave requests with it.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with a `PRESET_*` status;
     * [`DspiError::Locked`] (Link); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer error.
     *
     * Takes a confirmation token ([`crate::confirm`], WP P1TK; required since
     * WP P1TZ): [`DspiError::ConfirmationRequired`], with nothing sent,
     * without a good one. There is no form without a token.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun saveOutputConfigConfirmed(token: String?)

    /**
     * On the demo device only, for clients' tests: how
     * [`save_output_config`](Self::save_output_config_confirmed) answers from now on. 0 goes
     * through, 0xFF stalls, another byte is that status. `Unsupported`, with no
     * traffic, on anything but a simulated device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun demoOutputSaveStatus(status: Int)

    /**
     * The bus of output slot `slot` (a stereo pair, 0..4): 0 = S/PDIF,
     * 1 = I2S. The firmware switches later through a muted pipeline reset.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (no such slot)
     * or `INVALID_PIN` (a type above 1); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setOutputType(slot: Int, kind: Int)

    /**
     * The I2S bit-clock GPIO (the word clock is `pin + 1`). `role` 0 = the
     * master/shared pair, 1 = the slave pair used in split clock-pin mode.
     * `PIN_RESET_TO_DEFAULT` restores the role's default, read back.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (role above 1),
     * `INVALID_PIN`, `PIN_IN_USE` or `OUTPUT_ACTIVE` (I2S outputs run on
     * that pair); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`],
     * [`DspiError::Locked`] (Link, reset only) or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setI2sBckPin(role: Int, pin: Int)

    /**
     * 0 = master and slave share the clock pins (unified), 1 = separate
     * pairs (split).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_PARAM` (mode above 1),
     * `OUTPUT_ACTIVE` (a live swap under running I2S outputs) or
     * `PIN_IN_USE` (the slave pair is taken); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setI2sClockPinMode(mode: Int)

    /**
     * The I2S master clock output on or off.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `PIN_IN_USE` (its pin was taken
     * meanwhile); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a
     * transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setMckEnabled(enabled: Boolean)

    /**
     * The master clock GPIO (a clock-output-capable pin);
     * `PIN_RESET_TO_DEFAULT` restores the default, read back.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_PIN` (not a GPOUT pin),
     * `OUTPUT_ACTIVE` (MCK is enabled) or `PIN_IN_USE`;
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`],
     * [`DspiError::Locked`] (Link, reset only) or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setMckPin(pin: Int)

    /**
     * Master clock rate: 0 = 128 × fs, 1 = 256 × fs.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_PIN` for anything else;
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer
     * error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setMckMultiplier(mult: Int)

    /**
     * The ADAT lightpipe output (RP2350) on or off.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (RP2040),
     * `INVALID_PARAM`, or `INVALID_PIN` / `PIN_IN_USE` (its pin, on
     * enabling); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a
     * transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setAdatOutEnabled(enabled: Boolean)

    /**
     * The ADAT output GPIO; `PIN_RESET_TO_DEFAULT` restores the default,
     * read back. Allowed while enabled (the stream moves under mute).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (RP2040),
     * `INVALID_PIN` or `PIN_IN_USE`; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`], [`DspiError::Locked`] (Link, reset
     * only) or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setAdatOutPin(pin: Int)

    /**
     * The RX GPIO of S/PDIF input `index` (0..3); `PIN_RESET_TO_DEFAULT`
     * restores the default, read back. A disabled optional input stores
     * the pin as a preference; the live input is re-routed.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (no such input),
     * `INVALID_PIN` or `PIN_IN_USE`; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`], [`DspiError::Locked`] (Link, reset
     * only) or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setSpdifRxPin(index: Int, pin: Int)

    /**
     * Enable or disable optional S/PDIF input `index` (1..3). Input 0 is
     * always on: enabling it is a no-op, disabling it is refused. Bit 0 of
     * `DspState.spdif_inputs_enabled` therefore always stays set.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (index 0 off, or
     * no such input) or `PIN_IN_USE` (enabling: its pin is invalid or
     * taken; disabling: it is the live source or a pending switch targets
     * it, so switch the source away first); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setSpdifInputEnabled(index: Int, enabled: Boolean)

    /**
     * The data GPIO of I2S input stereo pair `pair` (0..3);
     * `PIN_RESET_TO_DEFAULT` restores the default, read back.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (no such pair),
     * `INVALID_PIN` or `PIN_IN_USE`; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`], [`DspiError::Locked`] (Link, reset
     * only) or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setI2sRxPin(pair: Int, pin: Int)

    /**
     * I2S input channels: 2, 4, 6 or 8 (four pairs on RP2350, one on
     * RP2040).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_PIN` (not 2/4/6/8),
     * `INVALID_OUTPUT` (more pairs than the part has) or a pin status for a
     * newly active pair; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setI2sInputChannels(channels: Int)

    /**
     * The ADAT input (RP2350) on or off. Enabling needs a data pin
     * (`DspState.adat_input_pin` not 0xFF); only an enabled ADAT input can
     * be selected as the source.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (RP2040),
     * `INVALID_PIN` (no pin set), or `PIN_IN_USE` (its pin is taken; or,
     * disabling, it is the live source: switch away first);
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer
     * error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setAdatInputEnabled(enabled: Boolean)

    /**
     * The ADAT input GPIO; 0xFF clears it (allowed only while the input is
     * disabled). The ADAT output's pin is allowed (loopback self-test).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_OUTPUT` (RP2040),
     * `INVALID_PIN` or `PIN_IN_USE` (taken, or clearing while enabled);
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer
     * error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setAdatInputPin(pin: Int)

    /**
     * ADAT input clock: 0 = master (the device sets the rate), 1 = slave
     * (follows the lightpipe). Applied later in the firmware's main loop;
     * read the live mode back with [`DspiCore::fetch_input_lock`] (1).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with `INVALID_PARAM` (above 1);
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer
     * error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setAdatInputClockMode(mode: Int)

    /**
     * I2S/ADAT-master input rate: 0 = 44.1k, 1 = 48k, 2 = 96k (others clamp
     * to 48k). REQ_SET_INPUT_RATE 0xED is a payload SET with no status byte:
     * Ok means the transfer completed. The firmware stores the rate and
     * applies it live only while it is the rate authority (I2S or ADAT input
     * in master clock mode). Sets `DspState.i2s_input_rate`.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a
     * transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setInputRate(index: Int)

    /**
     * Hz the pipeline runs at now (0 if unknown): the LIVE rate (firmware
     * `audio_state.freq`, REQ_GET_INPUT_RATE 0xEE word 0, same as status
     * wValue 15) for every source, not the configured I2S rate (word 1,
     * ignored; that one is `DspState.i2s_input_rate`).
     *
     * Errors: a reply shorter than 4 bytes or another transfer error;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchInputRate(): Long

    /**
     * I2S clock mode, 0 master / 1 slave. REQ_SET_I2S_CLOCK_MODE 0x88 is a
     * payload SET with no status byte (Ok = transfer completed); the
     * firmware applies it deferred in its main loop, and notifies then.
     * `DspState.i2s_clock_mode` holds the requested mode at once; the
     * firmware applies it later, and its notification brings the mirror to
     * the live mode.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a mode above 1 (the
     * firmware would ignore it silently); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setI2sClockMode(mode: Int)

    /**
     * Lock status of a clocked input. `which`: 0 = I2S slave
     * (`REQ_GET_I2S_SLAVE_STATUS`, 16 bytes; `rate_ok` is always true),
     * 1 = ADAT input (`REQ_GET_ADAT_INPUT_STATUS`, 20 bytes; all zero on
     * RP2040).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for another `which`; a short
     * reply or other transfer error; [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchInputLock(which: Int): InputLockStatus

    /**
     * The ADAT output's status (8 bytes; all zero on RP2040).
     *
     * Errors: a short reply or other transfer error;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchAdatOutStatus(): AdatOutStatus

    /**
     * Send a DAC hardware mute config: on/off, polarity (`active_low`:
     * pull the pin low to mute), GPIO, and the hold times in ms around a
     * clock stop. The firmware validates and applies it later in its main
     * loop (with a flash write) and drops an invalid one silently: `Ok`
     * means only that the transfer completed. Read the outcome back with
     * [`DspiCore::fetch_dac_mute`] about 150 ms later; the mirror is not
     * changed here.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a
     * transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setDacMute(enabled: Boolean, activeLow: Boolean, pin: Int, holdMs: Int, releaseMs: Int)

    /**
     * Re-read the DAC mute config into the state (`dac_mute_*`; also the
     * support probe: `dac_mute_supported` becomes true when it answers).
     *
     * Errors: a stall (firmware without the feature), a short reply or
     * another transfer error; [`DspiError::Disconnected`]. The mirror is
     * unchanged on error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchDacMute()

    /**
     * Pulse the mute output for about a second, so an installer can hear
     * that the pin and polarity are right.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] { 3 } (`INVALID_OUTPUT`) when the DAC
     * mute is disabled; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun testDacMute()

    /**
     * LG Sound Sync on or off: live, saved with the preset. Sets
     * `DspState.lg_sound_sync_enabled`.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a
     * transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setLgSoundSync(enabled: Boolean)

    /**
     * LG Sound Sync status: enabled, a TV detected, its last volume
     * (0..100, 0xFF before one is decoded) and mute.
     *
     * Errors: a short reply or other transfer error;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchLgStatus(): LgStatus

    /**
     * Re-read UART/I2C configs + status into the state (`uart`, `i2c`,
     * `ctrl_status`, `ctrl_iface_supported`). A device that does not
     * answer the status read has no control interfaces:
     * `ctrl_iface_supported` becomes false and the error is returned.
     *
     * Errors: a stall, a short reply or another transfer error;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchCtrlIfaces()

    /**
     * Send a UART control-interface config (pins, baud, notifications).
     * The firmware applies and persists it later; read the outcome
     * (`ctrl_status.uart_last_status`, a `PIN_CONFIG_*` code) with
     * [`DspiCore::fetch_ctrl_ifaces`] about 250 ms later. The mirror is not
     * changed here.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a
     * transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setUart(config: UartConfig)

    /**
     * Send an I2C target control-interface config (pins, 7-bit address).
     * Applied and persisted later, as [`DspiCore::set_uart`]; read the
     * outcome with [`DspiCore::fetch_ctrl_ifaces`].
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Disconnected`] or a
     * transfer error.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setI2c(config: I2cConfig)

    /**
     * Reboots the device into its USB bootloader (it disappears). Ok
     * whatever the transfer gives, as in the old core: the device drops off
     * the bus (also Ok without a device, unless tokens are required: see
     * Errors). Allowed on a read-only
     * connection, so a firmware too old or too new for this build can be
     * updated.
     *
     * The connection stays open until the departure is noticed (hotplug or
     * notifications). A firmware update then goes: this call; the
     * bootloader drive appears (`bootloader_drives`); `flash_confirmed`
     * writes the image; the DSPi re-enumerates. Only that last step
     * confirms success: open it again and check the new version with
     * [`DspiCore::firmware_version_matches`]; a timeout there is the
     * failure.
     *
     * The device lock is taken first and held across the restart (WP CG24),
     * so no other DSPi Link session writes to a device about to vanish; the
     * hub drops it when the device leaves, and the connection's close gives
     * it back; otherwise it lasts 60 s. [`DspiCore::device_lock_release`]
     * does not end it: an install that must end it early (a restart that
     * never happened) takes the caller's lock first
     * ([`DspiCore::device_lock_acquire`]), inside which this takes nothing
     * of its own, and releases that. A refused lock sends nothing.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Locked`] / [`DspiError::Denied`] when the Link
     * hub refuses its lock; [`DspiError::Disconnected`] when the connection
     * changed while the hub was asked;
     * [`DspiError::ConfirmationRequired`] without a good token, also with no
     * device open: the gate runs first, so that case is no longer the plain
     * `Ok` (accepted in review p1b).
     *
     * Takes a confirmation token ([`crate::confirm`], WP P1TK; required since
     * WP P1TZ); with no device open a token is still checked, and nothing is
     * sent. There is no form without a token.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun enterBootloaderConfirmed(token: String?)

    /**
     * 0 idle, 1 PDM, 2 EQ worker. Also stored in `DspState.core1_mode`.
     *
     * Errors: a stall or other transfer error;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchCore1Mode(): Int

    /**
     * Whether enabling `output` would conflict with Core 1's mode (PDM on
     * the last output and the Core 1 EQ worker exclude each other, and the
     * firmware skips a blocked enable silently). One `REQ_GET_CORE1_CONFLICT`
     * read; a failed read is an error, never "no conflict".
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an output past the
     * device's output count (`DspState.num_output_channels`);
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device; a stall, timeout or other
     * transfer error from the read.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun checkCore1Conflict(output: Int): Boolean

    /**
     * Register (`Some`) or clear (`None`) the arrival/departure listener.
     * Clearing waits for a call in progress on another thread. The
     * listener is called on the thread that calls
     * [`DspiCore::poll_hotplug`], without the core's lock held; it may call
     * back into the core (`scan_devices`, `is_connected`), but must not set
     * listeners from inside the callback.
     */
    fun setHotplugCallback(listener: DeviceEvents?)

    /**
     * Compare the USB bus with the last poll and report arrivals and
     * departures (by serial) to the hotplug listener; reconnect the
     * selected device when it reappears, and close the open device when it
     * departs or its notification stream ends. Desktop apps call it about
     * every 500 ms; it enumerates without opening anything.
     *
     * **Poll off the UI thread.** A reconnect runs inside this call: the
     * whole connect sequence (the probe, the bulk read, the notification
     * reader's start) blocks the polling thread for that poll, a few hundred
     * milliseconds on a replug, and longer if the device answers slowly.
     * The listener calls and the reconnect both happen on this thread.
     *
     * Call [`DspiCore::scan_devices`] before the first poll: the first poll
     * diffs against that scan, so without one every unit present is
     * announced as arrived.
     *
     * Never fails: a failed reconnect is logged and retried on the next
     * polls (a few times). On Android there is nothing to enumerate (the
     * app gets USB broadcasts instead); there it only closes a connection
     * whose stream ended, and apps need not call it.
     */
    fun pollHotplug()

    /**
     * Which copy of the limits, the installer PIN and the pinned modes the
     * Installer page edits for the open device: the hub's when the device
     * is reached through a hub that keeps them (editable by an Admin of the
     * hub only), this console's own when it is connected directly (USB,
     * MIDI, the demo), none otherwise. No I/O.
     */
    fun installerTarget(): InstallerTarget

    /**
     * The hub's limits profile, PIN state and pinned modes for the open
     * device (`limits.get`, `mode.pinned`). Every role may read them. Slow
     * (two round trips): off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `no_limits` when the device is not
     * reached through a hub that keeps limits (notice `dlink.no_limits`), or
     * the hub's failure; [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubLimitsGet(): HubAdminState

    /**
     * Set the hub's limits profile for the open device: `document` is a
     * limits document (or one carrying `meta.limits`). The hub holds the
     * device to it at once and says what it held. Admin of the hub only.
     * The lock is read again. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a document that is not a
     * limits profile; [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_limits_get`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubLimitsSet(document: String): HubLimitsSet

    /**
     * Remove the hub's limits profile for the open device (nothing on the
     * device changes; with it goes what the lock covers). Admin of the hub
     * only. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_limits_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubLimitsClear(): HubAdminState

    /**
     * Set (or change) the hub's installer PIN for the open device: the hub
     * keeps only its salted, slow hash, and makes a recovery code shown this
     * once. Every console's unlock of the device ends. Admin of the hub only
     * (an Admin needs no PIN). Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a PIN of fewer than 4 or
     * more than 64 characters, or with a space at either end; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_limits_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubPinSet(pin: String): HubPinSet

    /**
     * Remove the hub's installer PIN for the open device (and its recovery
     * code): the hub's lock is off. Admin of the hub only. Slow: off the UI
     * thread.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_limits_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubPinClear(): HubAdminState

    /**
     * Pin `role`'s mode on the hub (`viewer`, `control`, `admin`; `mode`
     * one of the manifest's modes, "" to unpin). Hub-wide: every console of
     * that role shows it. Admin of the hub only. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an unknown role or mode; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_limits_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubModePin(role: String, mode: String): HubAdminState

    /**
     * Register (`Some`) or clear (`None`) the listener for the hub's admin
     * events ([`HubAdminListener`]).
     */
    fun setHubAdminListener(listener: HubAdminListener?)

    /**
     * The hub's admin events since the last call, oldest first (at most
     * 32 are kept), for a binding that polls instead of a listener (the
     * web). No I/O.
     */
    fun hubAdminEvents(): List<HubAdminEvent>

    /**
     * Which of the hub's families this console's hub has, from its `hello`.
     * All false with no live DSPi Link session. No I/O.
     */
    fun hubCaps(): HubCaps

    /**
     * Every DSPi the hub has seen, online or not: online first, then by
     * last seen, newest first (`inventory.list`, read page by page, at most
     * 1024). Every role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `inventory` cap (nothing
     * sent); [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; the hub's refusal (module docs).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubInventory(): List<HubDevice>

    /**
     * Identify the open device: blink its LED for `seconds` (0 stops it;
     * at most 120) and, with `tone_output`, play its quiet identify tone
     * once on that output (leave it empty unless the user asked for sound).
     * On a hub with the `identify` cap this is the hub's `device.identify`
     * (Control or Admin; the hub sends it, at most once per device per 2 s);
     * connected directly it is the device's own `EXT_IDENTIFY`
     * ([`DspiCore::identify`]). The answer is the device's state read after
     * the write; `None` when that read failed (the write went out). Slow:
     * off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] (nothing sent) on a hub without the
     * cap and on firmware without `EXT_IDENTIFY`;
     * [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a tone output the device does not
     * have; [`DspiError::Locked`] for a tone under another session's lock;
     * [`DspiError::Link`] `rate_limited`, `offline`, `not_found`, `device`;
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] in read-only mode; as
     * [`DspiCore::identify`] directly.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubIdentify(seconds: Int, toneOutput: Int?): IdentifyInfo?

    /**
     * One health record per online device of the hub (`health.get`):
     * CPU, the clip latch, temperature, uptime, hangs, whether it answers;
     * -- WP P3HB -- the stream counters, clip events a minute and the last
     * change where the hub has them.
     * Every role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `health` cap;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; the hub's refusal.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubHealth(): List<HubHealth>

    /**
     * The hub's statistics (`hub.stats`): sessions, uptime, and per attached
     * device its commands, errors, mean time and dropped notifications.
     * Admin of the hub only. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `hub_stats` cap;
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin; [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubStats(): HubStats

    /**
     * Device `serial`'s history on the hub ("" the open device), with ids
     * above `after_id`, oldest first, at most `limit` (capped at 500; 0
     * is the hub's 100): with `after_id` 0 the newest page
     * (`event.list`). Every role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `events` cap;
     * [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found` for a device with no log on the hub;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubEventsRead(serial: String, afterId: Long, limit: Long): HubEventPage

    /**
     * Have the hub send each new record of device `serial`'s history ("":
     * every device) as `event.added` (`on`), or stop (`event.watch`). The
     * records reach [`HubServerListener`] and [`DspiCore::hub_server_events`].
     * A watch ends with the hub session: ask again after a reconnect. Every
     * role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_events_read`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubEventsWatch(on: Boolean, serial: String)

    /**
     * Register (`Some`) or clear (`None`) the listener for the hub's
     * `event.added`, `inventory.changed` and `snapshot.changed`.
     */
    fun setHubServerListener(listener: HubServerListener?)

    /**
     * The hub's events of these families since the last call, oldest first
     * (at most 64 are kept), for a binding that polls instead of a listener
     * (the web). No I/O.
     */
    fun hubServerEvents(): List<HubServerEvent>

    /**
     * Device `serial`'s snapshots on the hub ("" the open device), oldest
     * first (`snapshot.list`). Every role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `hub_snapshots` cap;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; the hub's refusal.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsList(serial: String): List<SnapshotInfo>

    /**
     * Snapshot `id` on the hub with its document (`snapshot.get`). Every
     * role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`; `conflict` for a document
     * over 61 440 bytes compact (the hub's words); as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_snapshots_list`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsGet(id: String): HubSnapshotDocument

    /**
     * File snapshot `document` (its text: a snapshot document of device
     * `serial`, "" for the serial its `snapshot` block names) on the hub
     * (`snapshot.put`), under the id the document carries. The same document
     * again is `created: false`. Control or Admin of the hub. Slow: off the
     * UI thread.
     *
     * A document over 61 440 bytes in its compact JSON form is refused here,
     * with nothing sent: [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict`, "a snapshot document
     * is at most 61440 bytes on the DSPi Link (compact JSON); this one is N",
     * as the hub would answer.
     *
     * Errors: that; [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for text that is not a
     * JSON object, or the hub's `bad_request` (not a snapshot of that
     * device); [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict` (the hub holds a different
     * snapshot with this id, or a cap is reached); [`DspiError::Denied`]
     * for a Viewer; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`]; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_snapshots_list`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsPut(serial: String, document: String): HubSnapshotPut

    /**
     * Rename snapshot `id` on the hub (`snapshot.rename`; at most 256
     * characters). Control or Admin. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_snapshots_put`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsRename(id: String, name: String): SnapshotInfo

    /**
     * Set snapshot `id`'s note on the hub (`snapshot.note`; at most 4096
     * characters). Control or Admin. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_snapshots_rename`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsNote(id: String, note: String): SnapshotInfo

    /**
     * Delete snapshot `id` from the hub (`snapshot.delete`). Admin of the
     * hub only. This console's own copy, if any, stays. Slow: off the UI
     * thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_snapshots_rename`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsDelete(id: String)

    /**
     * Recall snapshot `id` from the hub's store on device `serial` ("" the
     * open device), written by the hub as this console's session
     * (`snapshot.recall`). With `guard` a guarded apply begins first
     * (`timeout_ms`, 0 for the hub's 30 s): confirm it, or it reverts.
     * Control or Admin. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * WP P2CC3: a guarded recall is this core's guarded apply, as one begun
     * here: [`DspiCore::apply_status`] reports it (`hub: true`, the
     * recall's changes), [`DspiCore::apply_confirm`] keeps it and
     * [`DspiCore::apply_revert`] (or the timeout, through
     * [`DspiCore::apply_poll`]) has the hub put back what it captured, as
     * this session. While another guarded apply waits it is refused with
     * [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`], nothing sent.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::ImageRefused`] for Control on a device with a
     * limits profile or a lock (nothing written); [`DspiError::Link`]
     * `offline`, `not_found`, `conflict` (another session's guarded apply);
     * as [`DspiCore::hub_snapshots_put`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsRecall(serial: String, id: String, guard: Boolean, timeoutMs: Long): HubSnapshotRecall

    /**
     * "Save to the hub": file this console's snapshot `id` (its own store,
     * [`DspiCore::snapshot_set_dir`]) on the hub, under the same id and the
     * device it was taken from. Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_snapshots_put`],
     * and as [`DspiCore::snapshot_export`] for the local read.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsSave(id: String): HubSnapshotPut

    /**
     * "Copy here": file the hub's snapshot `id` in this console's own store.
     * Kept as the hub has it, under its capture-time id unless this store
     * already holds that id with other content (then the copy gets the
     * next free one: nothing is overwritten); the same snapshot already here
     * is answered as it is. Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_snapshots_get`], and
     * [`DspiError::Io`] with no folder named for snapshots.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSnapshotsCopyHere(id: String): SnapshotInfo

    /**
     * The hub's alarms (`alarm.list`): the device's and the audio link's,
     * those on first (newest first), then the cleared, at most 500.
     * `serial` "" for every alarm, else only that device's; `state` ""
     * (every one), `active` (raised or acknowledged) or `cleared`. Every
     * role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `alarms` cap (nothing
     * sent); [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for another `state`;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; the hub's refusal.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubAlarms(serial: String, state: String): List<HubAlarm>

    /**
     * Acknowledge alarm `id` (`alarm.ack`): the alarm as it is now. A
     * `device_restarted` or `schedule_failed` alarm is cleared by it.
     * Control or Admin. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found` for an alarm that is not on;
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] for a Viewer; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`]; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_alarms`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubAlarmAck(id: String): HubAlarm

    /**
     * The alarm rules (`alarm.rules.get`), one row per code. Every role.
     * Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_alarms`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubAlarmRulesGet(): List<HubAlarmRule>

    /**
     * Change the alarm rules (`alarm.rules.set`): the codes given, each
     * with `on` and the fields that are `Some` (the others keep the hub's
     * values); a code left out keeps its rule. All or nothing. The rules
     * now. Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a rule without a code or a
     * code given twice (nothing sent), and the hub's `bad_request` naming a
     * field out of range; [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin;
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`]; as [`DspiCore::hub_alarms`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubAlarmRulesSet(rules: List<HubAlarmRule>): List<HubAlarmRule>

    /**
     * The hub's schedule (`schedule.list`): the rules with their next and
     * last runs, the time zone, the hub's local time and whether its clock
     * is unset. Every role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `schedule` cap;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; the hub's refusal.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubSchedules(): HubSchedules

    /**
     * Add a rule (`id` "") or replace rule `id` whole (`schedule.set`); its
     * `next_run` and `last` are not sent. The rule as the hub lists it.
     * Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for both `days` and `once`, a
     * `param.set` without a value (nothing sent) and the hub's
     * `bad_request`; [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found` (no rule `id`),
     * `conflict` (256 rules); [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin;
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`]; as [`DspiCore::hub_schedules`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubScheduleSet(rule: HubScheduleRule): HubScheduleRule

    /**
     * Delete rule `id` (`schedule.delete`). Admin of the hub. Slow: off the
     * UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_schedule_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubScheduleDelete(id: String)

    /**
     * Run rule `id` now (`schedule.run_now`), whether it is enabled or not:
     * the hub writes its targets as `Schedule: <name>`, and the run's
     * results come back (one row per action and target; a failed row is not
     * a failed call). Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`, `conflict` while the hub's
     * clock is unset; as [`DspiCore::hub_schedule_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubScheduleRunNow(id: String): HubScheduleRun

    /**
     * The notification targets (`outbound.get`), secrets as `set` or `not
     * set`, with each one's queue and last try. Admin of the hub. Slow: off
     * the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `outbound` cap;
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin; [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubOutboundGet(): List<HubOutboundTarget>

    /**
     * Replace the notification targets (`outbound.set`, the whole list, at
     * most 16). A target with `secret` `new` gets a secret made by the hub:
     * in [`HubOutboundSet::secrets`], this once. The list as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_outbound_get`] gives can be sent back as it is. Admin
     * of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] with the hub's words naming the
     * entry and field (nothing kept); as [`DspiCore::hub_outbound_get`];
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubOutboundSet(targets: List<HubOutboundTarget>): HubOutboundSet

    /**
     * Send target `id` one delivery of kind `test` now (`outbound.test`),
     * recorded as its last try. Admin of the hub. Slow (up to 10 s): off
     * the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_outbound_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubOutboundTest(id: String): HubOutboundTest

    /**
     * The broker setting (`mqtt.get`): the values in use, the password and
     * a client key as `set` or `not set`. Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI
     * thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `mqtt` cap;
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin; [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMqttGet(): HubMqttSettings

    /**
     * Change the broker setting (`mqtt.set`): every field as given (what
     * [`DspiCore::hub_mqtt_get`] answered can be sent back as it is; see
     * [`HubMqttSettings`] for "" and the password). Any change but Home
     * Assistant's ends the connection and starts a new one. The setting
     * now. Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] with the hub's words naming the
     * field (nothing changed); as [`DspiCore::hub_mqtt_get`];
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMqttSet(settings: HubMqttSettings): HubMqttSettings

    /**
     * The broker connection now (`mqtt.status`). Admin of the hub. Slow: off
     * the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_mqtt_get`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMqttStatus(): HubMqttStatus

    /**
     * The hub's API keys (`auth.list`'s rows of kind `api`; the consoles'
     * pairings are left out). Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `api_keys` cap;
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin; [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubApiKeys(): List<HubApiKey>

    /**
     * Mint an API key `name` with `role` (`viewer`, `control`, `admin`;
     * never above this session's) for REST and Home Assistant
     * (`auth.mint`). The key is in this answer only: show it once. Admin of
     * the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for another role (nothing
     * sent) or the hub's `bad_request`; [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin or
     * for a role above this session's; [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict` with
     * 64 clients and keys; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`]; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_api_keys`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubApiKeyMint(name: String, role: String, plaintextOk: Boolean): HubApiKeyMinted

    /**
     * Revoke API key `cid` (`auth.revoke`): its REST session ends. Admin of
     * the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a `cid` that is not an API
     * key's here (nothing sent, so a console's pairing is never revoked by
     * this call) or that the hub does not know; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_api_key_mint`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubApiKeyRevoke(cid: Long)

    /**
     * The hub's groups, by id (`group.list`). Every role. Slow: off the UI
     * thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `groups` cap (nothing
     * sent); [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; the hub's refusal.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroups(): List<HubGroup>

    /**
     * Store `group` on the hub (`group.set`): without an id a new group,
     * with one that group replaced whole (an empty note clears it). The
     * hub's bounds: a name of 1 to 48 characters, a note of at most 256,
     * at most 64 members, each an inventory serial and each once; 64 groups.
     * Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] past a bound (`bad_request`);
     * [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found` (a member not in the inventory, an
     * unknown id), `conflict` (a 65th group); [`DspiError::Denied`] below
     * Admin; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] (nothing sent); as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_groups`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroupSet(group: HubGroup): HubGroupSet

    /**
     * Delete group `id` (`group.delete`; its id is never given again).
     * Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an empty id (nothing sent);
     * [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`; as [`DspiCore::hub_group_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroupDelete(id: String)

    /**
     * Read `path` (`idx` its indices) on every device of `target`
     * (`group.param.get`): one row per member, in member order, each with
     * its value or its code. Every role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `group_params` cap
     * (nothing sent); [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a target that is
     * both or neither (nothing sent) and the hub's `bad_request`;
     * [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found` (the group), `busy` (this session's
     * other group request runs); [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroupParamGet(target: HubFleetTarget, path: String, idx: List<Long>): HubGroupParams

    /**
     * Write `value` to `path` on every device of `target`, one after
     * another as this console's child session (`group.param.set`): each
     * judged on its own by role, limits and lock; a refused row stops no
     * other. `offsets` (dB paths only) add to the value per member, clamped
     * into that device's range (`clamped` in its row). Control or Admin.
     * Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_group_param_get`];
     * [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a structured value (nothing sent),
     * a value outside the registry's range, offsets on a path not in dB;
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] for a Viewer; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] (nothing
     * sent).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroupParamSet(target: HubFleetTarget, path: String, idx: List<Long>, value: ParamValue, offsets: List<HubOffset>): HubGroupParams

    /**
     * Load a preset on every device of `target` (`group.preset.load`): by
     * `slot` (0 to 9) or by `name`, one of the two; `map` gives a member
     * its own. One row per member, with the slot and name loaded. Control
     * or Admin. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for both or neither of slot
     * and name (nothing sent); as [`DspiCore::hub_group_param_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroupPresetLoad(target: HubFleetTarget, slot: Int?, name: String, map: List<HubPresetPick>): List<HubGroupRow>

    /**
     * Apply one snapshot to every device of `target` (`group.apply`):
     * exactly one of `snapshot` (an id in the hub's store), `name` (one
     * snapshot's name in the store) or `document` (a snapshot document's
     * text, at most 61 440 bytes compact). Each member is the hub's recall
     * as this console's child session. With `guard`, one guarded apply per
     * device (`timeout_ms` 1000 to 600 000; 0 for the hub's 30 s): confirm
     * the run ([`DspiCore::hub_group_apply_confirm`]) or each reverts at its
     * deadline. Control or Admin. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a source that is not
     * exactly one, or a document that is no JSON object (nothing sent);
     * [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict` for a document over the wire cap
     * (nothing sent) and two snapshots of that name; `not_found`; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_group_param_set`] (cap `group_apply`).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroupApply(target: HubFleetTarget, snapshot: String, name: String, document: String, guard: Boolean, timeoutMs: Long): HubGroupRun

    /**
     * Keep every pending guard of bulk-apply run `run`
     * (`group.apply.confirm`): one row per guard, `conflict` for one that
     * had already reverted. Only the session that ran it. Control or Admin.
     * Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found` (an unknown run, or another
     * session's); as [`DspiCore::hub_group_apply`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroupApplyConfirm(run: String): HubGroupRun

    /**
     * Revert every pending guard of run `run` now (`group.apply.cancel`):
     * one row per guard, `reverted` set. Errors: as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_group_apply_confirm`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubGroupApplyCancel(run: String): HubGroupRun

    /**
     * The hub's firmware images (`fw.image.list`). Every role, over an
     * encrypted session only. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `rollout` cap (nothing
     * sent); [`DspiError::Link`] `tls_required` over a plaintext session;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubFirmwareImages(): HubFirmwareImages

    /**
     * Put firmware image `bytes` in the hub's store as `name` (`fw.image.put`,
     * 36 KiB a chunk, one upload per session). The hub checks the whole
     * image on the last chunk (`uf2_inspect`, the manifest by name and
     * bytes). Nothing goes over a plaintext session: the store is read first
     * (its `tls_required`). The bytes are never logged. Admin of the hub.
     * Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for no bytes or more than
     * [`FIRMWARE_MAX_BYTES`] (nothing sent), and the hub's `bad_request` (a
     * name or image it refuses); [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict` (the name
     * with other bytes; a fifth image), `busy` (an upload is open);
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] (nothing
     * sent); as [`DspiCore::hub_firmware_images`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubFirmwarePut(name: String, bytes: List<Int>): HubFirmwarePut

    /**
     * Delete image `name` from the hub's store (`fw.image.delete`). Admin
     * of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`, `conflict` (a running
     * rollout uses it); as [`DspiCore::hub_firmware_put`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubFirmwareDelete(name: String)

    /**
     * Start a firmware rollout of stored image `image` on `target`: one
     * serial, or a group (one member after another, stopped at the first
     * failure) (`fw.rollout.start`). The device must be muted unless
     * `while_live`. The answer comes after the first device's check and
     * guard steps; follow the rest with `fw.rollout.changed`
     * ([`super::HubServerEvent::rollout`]) or [`DspiCore::hub_rollouts`].
     * Admin of the hub, over an encrypted session. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors (nothing touched): [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a target
     * that is not one serial or one group (nothing sent);
     * [`DspiError::Unsupported`] (not on the hub's own USB);
     * [`DspiError::Locked`]; [`DspiError::ConfirmationRequired`] (not
     * muted); [`DspiError::ImageRefused`] (another platform, not verified,
     * the firmware guard's refusal); [`DspiError::Link`] `offline`,
     * `conflict`, `busy`, `not_found`, `tls_required`; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_firmware_put`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubRolloutStart(target: HubFleetTarget, image: String, whileLive: Boolean): HubRollout

    /**
     * The hub's rollouts, newest first (`fw.rollout.status`; the hub keeps
     * 64), or only rollout `id` ("" for all). Every role, over an encrypted
     * session. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found` for an unknown id; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_firmware_images`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubRollouts(id: String): List<HubRollout>

    /**
     * Cancel rollout `id` (`fw.rollout.cancel`): before the bootloader entry
     * of the device it is working on, never during a flash. The record as
     * it is now. Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict` (past the entry, or ended),
     * `not_found`; as [`DspiCore::hub_rollout_start`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubRolloutCancel(id: String): HubRollout

    /**
     * Set device `serial`'s location, note and tags in the hub's inventory
     * ("" the open device; `inventory.set`). Each `Some` replaces its field
     * and an empty one clears it (`Some("")`, `Some(vec![])`); `None` keeps
     * it. The hub's bounds: a location and a note at most 256 characters, no
     * control characters (a note may hold newlines and tabs); at most 16
     * tags of 1 to 32 characters, trimmed, duplicates dropped. The answer is
     * the record as the hub now has it. Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI
     * thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `inventory` cap (nothing
     * sent); [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a value past a bound (the
     * hub's `bad_request`, nothing changed); [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`
     * for a serial not in the inventory; [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin;
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] (nothing sent); [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubInventorySet(serial: String, location: String?, note: String?, tags: List<String>?): HubDevice

    /**
     * Forget device `serial` on the hub (`inventory.forget`): its record and
     * its history files, and with `with_data` its snapshots on the hub too.
     * Only an offline device: the hub refuses one that is attached. Admin of
     * the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an empty `serial` (nothing
     * sent); [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict` while the device is online,
     * `not_found` for a serial not in the inventory, `internal` when a file
     * could not be removed (the record stays: try again); as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_inventory_set`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubInventoryForget(serial: String, withData: Boolean): HubInventoryForgot

    /**
     * Rename the hub (`hub.rename`): control characters dropped and the ends
     * trimmed by the hub, at most 64 characters. The answer is the name the
     * hub now uses (in every later `hello`, `/dspi/v1/info` and its mDNS
     * record). Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `hub_stats` cap
     * (nothing sent); [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a name empty after
     * that or too long (nothing changed); [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin;
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] (nothing sent); [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubRename(name: String): String

    /**
     * Identify device `serial` on the hub ("" the open device;
     * `device.identify`): blink its LED for `seconds` (0 stops it; at most
     * 120, longer is shortened) and, with `tone_output`, play its quiet
     * identify tone once on that output (leave it empty unless the user
     * asked for sound). Any device on the hub, open here or not; the hub
     * sends it, at most once per device per 2 s, whoever asked. The answer
     * is the device's state read after the write; `None` when that read
     * failed (the write went out). Control or Admin of the hub. Slow: off
     * the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] (nothing sent) on no hub, on a hub
     * without the `identify` cap, and on firmware without `EXT_IDENTIFY`;
     * [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a tone output the device does not
     * have; [`DspiError::Locked`] for a tone under another session's lock;
     * [`DspiError::Link`] `rate_limited`, `offline`, `not_found`, `device`;
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] for a Viewer; [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] (nothing
     * sent); [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubIdentifySerial(serial: String, seconds: Int, toneOutput: Int?): IdentifyInfo?

    /**
     * The hub's documents of `kind` (`measurement`, `mic`, `target`; ""
     * every kind), oldest first within a kind (`measure.doc.list`): the
     * measurements of device `serial` ("" the open device's; with kind ""
     * and no device open, none), every microphone and target. Every role.
     * Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an unknown kind;
     * [`DspiError::Unsupported`] without the `hub_measure` cap (nothing
     * sent); [`DspiError::Disconnected`] (also for kind `measurement`, serial
     * "" and no device open); the hub's refusal.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMeasureDocs(kind: String, serial: String): List<MeasureDocInfo>

    /**
     * Document `id` on the hub with its text (`measure.doc.get`): a large
     * one part by part, the whole checked against the hub's size and hash
     * and read by its kind's reader. Every role. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`; `protocol` for an answer
     * that does not make the document it announced; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_measure_docs`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMeasureDocGet(id: String): HubMeasureDocument

    /**
     * File `document` (a measurement, microphone or target document's text)
     * on the hub (`measure.doc.put`), under the id it carries. It is read
     * and bounded here first (a text that is not such a document is refused
     * with nothing sent); a measurement without a serial takes the open
     * device's, a document without a store id the one a store would make
     * first (from its time). Sent whole up to 61 440 bytes compact, in parts
     * past it. The same document again is `created: false`. Control or
     * Admin of the hub. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a text that is not such a
     * document or past its bound (nothing sent), or the hub's
     * `bad_request`; [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict` (the hub holds another
     * document with this id, or a cap is reached) or `busy` (too many
     * uploads on the hub); [`DspiError::Denied`] for a Viewer;
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`]; as [`DspiCore::hub_measure_docs`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMeasureDocPut(document: String): HubMeasureDocPut

    /**
     * Rename document `id` on the hub (`measure.doc.rename`; any Unicode, at
     * most 256 characters). Control or Admin. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `not_found`; [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`]
     * past 256 characters (the hub's `bad_request`); as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_measure_doc_put`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMeasureDocRename(id: String, name: String): MeasureDocInfo

    /**
     * Set document `id`'s note on the hub (`measure.doc.note`; any Unicode,
     * at most 4096 characters). Control or Admin. Slow: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::hub_measure_doc_rename`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMeasureDocNote(id: String, note: String): MeasureDocInfo

    /**
     * Delete document `id` from the hub (`measure.doc.delete`). Admin of the
     * hub only. This console's own copy, if any, stays. Slow: off the UI
     * thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Denied`] below Admin; as
     * [`DspiCore::hub_measure_doc_rename`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun hubMeasureDocDelete(id: String)

    /**
     * The documents of `kind` (as [`DspiCore::measure_docs`]) on both sides:
     * this console's store and, on a DSPi Link session to a hub that keeps
     * them, the hub's ([`DspiCore::hub_measure_docs`]; its measurements are
     * device `serial`'s, "" the open device's). Each row says where it is
     * (`local`, `hub` or `both`). **The hub's wins:** an id both sides hold
     * with different content (any field of the entry differs) is listed as
     * the hub's, and this console's as a local copy beside it; nothing is
     * overwritten. A hub that cannot be read leaves the local rows, with
     * `hub_notice_key` saying why. A round trip on a hub: off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an unknown kind.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureDocsMerged(kind: String, serial: String): MeasureDocsMerged

    /**
     * The user's name for input source `source` (`REQ_GET_INPUT_SOURCE_NAME`,
     * registry `in.name`); "" when unnamed.
     *
     * Errors: `Unsupported` on firmware without source names (nothing is
     * sent); `InvalidArgument` for a source the device does not have;
     * `Disconnected` without a device or before it was probed; `Transfer`
     * (or `Denied` / `Locked` over DSPi Link) when the read fails.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun getInputSourceName(source: Int): String

    /**
     * Name input source `source` (`REQ_SET_INPUT_SOURCE_NAME`, registry
     * `in.name`); "" clears it. At most 31 bytes of UTF-8, no NUL. The
     * device stores it in flash from its main loop with a brief output
     * restart, then notifies `NOTIFY_EVT_INPUT_SOURCE_NAME`; a read right
     * after the write already returns the new name.
     *
     * Errors: `InvalidArgument` for a name that is too long or holds a NUL,
     * or a source the device does not have (nothing is sent); `ReadOnly` on
     * a read-only connection; `Unsupported` on firmware without source
     * names; `Disconnected`; `Transfer`, `Denied` or `Locked` from the link.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setInputSourceName(source: Int, name: String)

    /**
     * The device's own name (`REQ_GET_DEVICE_NAME`, registry `dev.name`), ""
     * when unnamed. Errors as for [`DspiCore::get_input_source_name`]
     * (`Unsupported` on firmware without device names).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun getDeviceName(): String

    /**
     * Rename the device (`REQ_SET_DEVICE_NAME`, registry `dev.name`); ""
     * clears it. At most 31 bytes of UTF-8, no NUL; the front panel shows
     * only printable ASCII. Applied and notified (`NOTIFY_EVT_DEVICE_NAME`)
     * as a source name. Errors as for [`DspiCore::set_input_source_name`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setDeviceName(name: String)

    /**
     * The name of input source `source` as last known, with no traffic: read
     * on connect, kept current by notifications (from any client) and by
     * [`DspiCore::set_input_source_name`], and read again after a lost
     * notification. "" when unnamed. For a client's rows, which would
     * otherwise read over the wire what connect has just read.
     *
     * Errors: `Unsupported` on firmware without source names; `InvalidArgument`
     * for a source past the table; `Disconnected` without a device or before
     * it was probed.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun cachedInputSourceName(source: Int): String

    /**
     * The device's name as last known, with no traffic, kept as
     * [`DspiCore::cached_input_source_name`] keeps source names. "" when
     * unnamed. Errors: `Unsupported` on firmware without device names;
     * `Disconnected`.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun cachedDeviceName(): String

    /**
     * Have someone else rename something on the demo device, for clients'
     * tests and demo mode: `target` is an input source, or 0xFF for the device
     * itself. The rename arrives as a notification that is not this client's
     * own. `Unsupported`, with no traffic, on anything but a simulated device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun demoRenameElsewhere(target: Int, name: String)

    /**
     * Set how the demo device applies an output-latency request, for clients'
     * tests: `mode` 0 at once (and a request held so far applies on the next
     * status read), 1 after `reads` status reads, 2 never, 3 dropped after
     * `reads` status reads. `blackout` status reads stall first while one is
     * pending, as the firmware's flash window does. `Unsupported`, with no
     * traffic, on anything but a simulated device; `InvalidArgument` for
     * another `mode`.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun demoLatencyTiming(mode: Int, reads: Int, blackout: Int)

    /**
     * How many output-latency requests the demo device has received, for
     * clients' tests that a wait never sends one again. `Unsupported`, with no
     * traffic, on anything but a simulated device.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun demoLatencySets(): Long

    /**
     * `REQ_OUTPUT_LATENCY` status (`wValue` 0, 12 bytes): the requested
     * and the effective option (a stream-only option requested while USB is
     * the source falls back to the first option without that flag), the
     * ring target in µs and frames, the batch in frames, `pending` (1 until
     * a SET has fully applied) and the option count, as `fields` in the
     * firmware's order.
     *
     * Errors: `Unsupported` on firmware without the setting (nothing is
     * sent); `Disconnected`; `Transfer` for a short or failed read.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchOutputLatency(): OutputLatency

    /**
     * The output-latency options (`REQ_OUTPUT_LATENCY` `wValue` 1), in the
     * firmware's order, with what choosing each gives on the active source
     * and rate (`effective_us`; a stream-only option on USB gives the
     * fallback's latency). A trailing partial entry is ignored.
     *
     * Errors as for [`DspiCore::fetch_output_latency`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchOutputLatencyOptions(): List<OutputLatencyOption>

    /**
     * Request output-latency option `id` (`OutputLatencyId`, 1..=7: 0.67,
     * 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 8 ms; registry `out.latency`). The device applies
     * it from its main loop (a directory write and a synchronized output
     * restart): until then [`DspiCore::fetch_output_latency`] reports the
     * old `requested_id` with `pending` 1. A stream-only option (0.67 and
     * 1 ms) is stored as asked, but USB input runs the fallback.
     *
     * Errors: `InvalidArgument` for an id that is not an option (nothing is
     * sent); `ReadOnly` on a read-only connection; `Unsupported` on
     * firmware without the setting; `Disconnected`; `Transfer`, `Denied`
     * or `Locked` from the link.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setOutputLatency(id: Int)

    /**
     * `REQ_GET_CLOCK_DIAG` (0x82): the `ClockDiagPacket` (clock_diag.h)
     * latched by the firmware's 1 Hz poll, decoded into `fields` in packet
     * order (`slot_*[i]` per S/PDIF slot; `_cppm` are ppm × 100, `_mhz`
     * Hz × 1000, `_q16` Q16.16 samples per frame). Decoding stops at the
     * end of the reply, so an older packet version simply has fewer fields.
     *
     * `available` is false, with `raw` and `fields` empty, on firmware
     * built without `DSPI_CLOCK_DIAG` (a zero-length reply), when the
     * packet does not fit the transport, or on firmware without the
     * opcode (it stalls). Errors: `Disconnected`; `Transfer`, `Denied` or
     * `Locked` from the link.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun fetchClockDiag(): ClockDiag

    /**
     * Step back: write back the value the newest undoable change replaced
     * (then what the device moved with it), through the typed setter for
     * its path. Changes that cannot be undone are stepped over and named in
     * [`HistoryMove::skipped`]. Nothing to undo is not an error: `entry` is
     * then `None`.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device, or when the
     * connection changed during the call (nothing is sent to another
     * device); the setter's errors, after which the change stays to be
     * undone.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun undo(): HistoryMove

    /**
     * Step forward: write again the change [`DspiCore::undo`] most recently
     * reversed. A new change since then ends what can be redone.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::undo`]; after a setter's error the change
     * stays to be redone.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun redo(): HistoryMove

    /**
     * Mark a continuous drag (a slider or a graph handle held and moved):
     * `true` when it starts, `false` when it ends. Between the two, every
     * write undo can reverse joins a single undo step, however long the
     * drag lasts and however many values it moves (several bands, a band's
     * fields, a slider per tick); undo puts each back as it was when the
     * drag began. A write outside a drag (a typed value, a key step, a
     * click) is always a step of its own, and one undo cannot reverse ends
     * the step. Each call ends the step being gathered, so two drags are two
     * steps. A drag ends with its connection: after a disconnect, another
     * device opened or a re-attach, writes are steps of their own until the
     * next `true`; with no device the mark is ignored. A client that never
     * calls this gets one step per write.
     */
    fun setDragging(dragging: Boolean)

    fun beginGroup(label: String)

    fun endGroup()

    /**
     * Whether [`DspiCore::undo`] has a change to reverse. False with no
     * device.
     */
    fun canUndo(): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether [`DspiCore::redo`] has a change to apply again. False with no
     * device.
     */
    fun canRedo(): Boolean

    /**
     * The newest `n` changes of this connection, newest first: every typed
     * setter's, undo's and redo's, with the value before and after, who
     * made it and when. The connection's session keeps the newest 1000; a
     * reconnect starts with none. Empty with no device.
     */
    fun history(n: Long): List<HistoryEntry>

    /**
     * Keep limits profiles (and their PIN hashes) in files under `dir`, the
     * app's own data folder, one subfolder per device serial (created when
     * first written; "" forgets the folder). A profile set before a folder
     * was named is kept for this core's lifetime only. Name it before opening
     * a device: with a device open whose installer PIN is set, this console
     * must be unlocked for it. Every installer PIN this core knows stays in
     * force, and is written to the new folder when that holds none for the
     * device (WP P1DKFIX).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on the web (no files: use
     * [`DspiCore::limits_apply_profile`] with the document the page keeps);
     * [`DspiError::InstallerLock`] `pin_required` while the open device's
     * PIN is set and this console is not unlocked (nothing changed).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun limitsSetDir(dir: String)

    /**
     * The open device's profile as a document (`meta.kind = "limits"`;
     * never a PIN), "" when it has none.
     */
    fun limitsProfileDocument(): String

    /**
     * Set the open device's profile from `document` (a limits document, or
     * any document carrying `meta.limits`), keep it (files when a folder is
     * named), and hold the device to it at once: a value outside a narrowed
     * limit is written to the limit (journalled, one undo group), and
     * reported. The profile's serial is the open device's.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device;
     * [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a document with no readable
     * profile; [`DspiError::Io`] when the file could not be written
     * (nothing changed).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun limitsApplyProfile(document: String): LimitsApply

    /**
     * Remove the open device's profile (its files too): the device runs as
     * it does with none. The PIN, if one is set, stays (P1LK).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device; [`DspiError::Io`]
     * when a file could not be removed.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun limitsClear()

    /**
     * The open device's limits: whether a profile is in force, how it was
     * found, the standing notice when its file is unreadable, and what the
     * last clamp held. Reads the profile's file once per device.
     */
    fun limitsStatus(): LimitsStatus

    /**
     * The open device's installer lock on this console: whether a PIN is
     * set, whether the locked-user state is on, what it covers and allows,
     * the refusal window after wrong PINs, the installer's note and contact.
     * With no device: no PIN, nothing locked.
     */
    fun installerLockState(): LockState

    /**
     * Unlock the open device on this console for this session with the
     * installer's PIN or the recovery code. With no PIN set, nothing is
     * asked and nothing changes. Slow (a PIN hash): off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device;
     * [`DspiError::InstallerLock`] `wrong_pin`, or `wait` while the refusal
     * window after repeated wrong PINs is open (nothing checked;
     * [`LockState::retry_after_ms`]).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun installerUnlock(pin: String): LockState

    /**
     * Lock the open device again on this console (the end of an unlock).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; [`DspiError::InstallerLock`]
     * `no_pin` when no PIN is set (nothing to lock).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun installerLock(): LockState

    /**
     * Set (or change) the open device's installer PIN on this console, and
     * make a new recovery code, shown this once. The console stays unlocked
     * for the device. Kept in the limits folder (`limits_set_dir`), else for
     * this core's lifetime. Slow (two hashes): off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`]
     * for a PIN of fewer than 4 or more than 64 characters, or with a space
     * at either end; [`DspiError::InstallerLock`] `pin_required` while a PIN
     * is set and the console is not unlocked; [`DspiError::Io`] when a file
     * could not be written; [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on the web.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun installerPinSet(pin: String): InstallerPin

    /**
     * Remove the open device's installer PIN (and its recovery code) on
     * this console: no lock, no prompt, as before a PIN was set. Nothing
     * to do with none set.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; [`DspiError::InstallerLock`]
     * `pin_required` while the console is not unlocked; [`DspiError::Io`]
     * when a file could not be removed.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun installerPinClear(): LockState

    /**
     * Choose what the open device's lock covers: screen ids of the mode
     * manifest (`front` and the rest; empty: the PIN locks nothing). Kept in
     * the device's limits profile (`lockedPages`), which is created when
     * there is none; the device is held to the profile as
     * [`DspiCore::limits_apply_profile`] does.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for
     * an id the manifest does not have; [`DspiError::InstallerLock`]
     * `pin_required`; [`DspiError::Io`] as `limits_apply_profile`.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun installerLockCover(pages: List<String>): LockState

    /**
     * Read the lock of the device bound on this console's DSPi Link session
     * from its hub (`limits.get`, and the mode the hub pins for this role:
     * `mode.pinned`), and return the one lock state the console shows: the
     * hub's when the hub holds a PIN for the device (`held_by` `hub`; then
     * [`DspiCore::installer_unlock`] asks the hub and this console's own PIN
     * asks nothing), this console's otherwise. Call it after binding a hub
     * device and when the hub says its limits changed. With no hub session,
     * or a hub without `limits`, nothing is asked: the console's own lock.
     * Slow (a round trip): off the UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] when the hub could not answer.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun installerLockRefresh(): LockState

    /**
     * Open a DSPi Link session to `url` (`link_ws_url`), replacing any
     * previous one. `token` = the token stored for this hub's id ("" =
     * none). `expected_hub_id` = that id. With it, a token goes out only after
     * the hello has named that hub, and another hub at the address fails the
     * call with `wrong_hub`, having seen no token. "" = not known: the token
     * goes to whichever hub answers (a hub added by address, no stored id).
     * Blocks for the hello and authentication (up to ~15 s). When
     * `needs_pairing`, ask for the PIN and call `link_pair`; otherwise the
     * device list is ready (`link_devices`).
     *
     * From here on the core keeps the session up by itself: see
     * [`LinkListener`] and `link_status`. An app does not redial.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`], its `code` being `wrong_hub`, `io`, `handshake`,
     * `hub_busy` (all the hub's sessions are in use; try again in a few
     * seconds), `handshake_refused` (trying again will not help),
     * `rate_limited` (the token stays good; wait a minute), `protocol`,
     * `timeout`, `closed`, or the hub's own.
     *
     * -- WP SECCORE -- `token` is the stored **credential** (PLAN-SEC 4.3): a
     * bare token (`v1`) or a `v2` string with the hub's key pin, as
     * `link_pair`, [`LinkListener::on_link_token_issued`] and
     * `link_security().credential` give it; store those whenever they change.
     * TLS is tried first. A pinned hub is reached over TLS only, with its key
     * checked before the token goes out: `hub_key_changed` when it is another
     * key (pair again), `hub_key_unverified` for a pairing link's pin,
     * `tls_refused` when TLS fails; never plaintext. -- WP PINTLS -- A record
     * from [`link_credential_unpaired`] (a hub pinned before, after Pair
     * Again) is TLS only too, any key, pinned by the pairing:
     * `tls_required_pinned` when TLS fails, before any PIN goes out. An unpinned hub without
     * TLS (an upstream hub) connects unencrypted, as before
     * (`link_security().encrypted` false). Same as
     * [`DspiCore::link_connect_advertised`] with no advert.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun linkConnect(url: String, clientName: String, appVersion: String, token: String, expectedHubId: String): LinkHello

    /**
     * [`DspiCore::link_connect`] with what the hub's DNS-SD advert said:
     * `tls_advertised` (the browsed record's `tls_advertised`, spec 3.1
     * `tls=1`): TLS is then required even without a pin, and its failure is
     * `tls_refused`, never a plaintext retry (ratchet (a)). A pinned hub needs
     * TLS whatever the advert says.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun linkConnectAdvertised(url: String, clientName: String, appVersion: String, credential: String, expectedHubId: String, tlsAdvertised: Boolean): LinkHello

    /**
     * The current session's encryption, for the hub's status and settings.
     */
    fun linkSecurity(): LinkSecurity

    /**
     * Pair with the PIN the user typed (role `control`, as the macOS
     * Console asks). Returns the token to store against the hub id ("" when
     * the hub issued none). If the hub dropped the socket while the prompt
     * was up (its 30 s auth deadline), it is dialled again first. A pairing
     * succeeds even if the device list then fails; the core reads it again.
     *
     * Errors, as [`DspiError::Link`] with `msg` ready to show:
     * - `wrong_pin`: a wrong PIN, or, on a hub showing its PIN in a window,
     * one that is wrong or expired, or a closed window (the hub does not
     * say which). Ask again;
     * - `rate_limited`: too many wrong tries; the hub refuses for a minute;
     * - `timeout`: no answer. The hub may still have accepted the PIN:
     * [`LinkListener::on_link_token_issued`] then still delivers the token;
     * - anything else [`DspiCore::link_connect`] reports;
     * - [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no session.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun linkPair(pin: String): String

    /**
     * The hub's devices (empty before authentication).
     */
    fun linkDevices(): List<LinkDevice>

    /**
     * Where the session stands; [`LinkStatus::phase`] is what to tell the
     * user. No blocking beyond the core lock.
     */
    fun linkStatus(): LinkStatus

    /**
     * Bind the core to the hub's device `handle` (as `open_fd` does for
     * USB): starts the notify listener over Link, resets the state. Then
     * `fetch_all` as usual (single-shot bulk, the hub's snapshot first).
     * Selecting again uses the same hub session and slot.
     *
     * The core binds it again by itself after a reconnect, and when the
     * hub lists it online again, unless the app called `disconnect` while
     * the session was up. `disconnect` while reconnecting does not stop
     * that: `link_close` does.
     *
     * Opening another device over the bound one closes the hub session. After `disconnect` the session stays, ready
     * for another `link_select_device`, and keeps its slot: call
     * `link_close` when leaving the hub.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a handle over 255 (they
     * are one byte on the wire); [`DspiError::Disconnected`] when the
     * session is not ready; as for the connect sequence.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun linkSelectDevice(handle: Long, serial: String)

    /**
     * Close the Link session (normal close) and drop a device bound to it,
     * and stop any reconnecting. This is how an app leaves a hub, giving back
     * its session slot (`disconnect` keeps the session, for another
     * `link_select_device`), and it is the banner's abort button.
     *
     * Safe on a UI thread: it returns within about 100 ms whatever the link
     * does, and `link_status` then says [`LinkPhase::Idle`]. It does not need
     * the core lock, and ends a command stuck on a stalled link. On a healthy
     * link the device is closed when it returns; on a stalled one that
     * finishes behind it. A dial in progress is thrown away. A USB device open
     * meanwhile stays open. Quiet: no `on_link_closed`.
     */
    fun linkClose()

    /**
     * Register (`Some`) or clear (`None`) the session events listener. Same
     * rules as the other listeners: called with the core lock released, and
     * clearing waits for a call in progress.
     */
    fun setLinkListener(listener: LinkListener?)

    /**
     * Try to reconnect now instead of waiting out the backoff (the platform
     * says the network is back). A no-op unless reconnecting.
     */
    fun linkRetryNow()

    /**
     * The hub answers at a new address (the app's browser found its `hid`
     * again after a DHCP change): reconnect there from now on, at once if
     * reconnecting. The core does not browse by itself.
     *
     * An address that cannot be dialled is logged and ignored: reconnecting
     * to it would fail for ever.
     */
    fun linkSetUrl(url: String)

    /**
     * Register (`Some`) or clear (`None`) the measurement listener: called
     * when a session's phase changes, it stops, or it asks something. Called
     * on the thread that pushed, ticked or stopped, with every core lock
     * released; it may read `measure_state` but must not block.
     */
    fun measureSetListener(listener: MeasureListener?)

    /**
     * What stands between `setup` and a measurement, in words (PLAN-P5
     * section 1.2): the volume (S2), the tweeter rule (S6), silence on the
     * inputs (S8), locks and roles (S11), processing the maths does not model
     * (S13), the plan's bounds (S7). Reads the device (the meters and the
     * generator's caps); writes nothing.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for
     * a setup that is not one (no outputs, an output twice, a count out of
     * range); a transfer failure of the reads.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measurePreflight(setup: MeasureSetup): MeasurePreflight

    /**
     * Start a measurement of `setup`: the preflight, the device lock
     * "Measuring" (a 30 s lease, renewed by `measure_tick`), then the
     * pre-roll. Nothing plays before the client has pushed the pre-roll and
     * ticked. The client then pushes capture whenever `wants_capture` and
     * calls `measure_tick` about every 50 ms until the phase is `done`,
     * `aborted` or `failed`.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a bad setup, a session
     * already running on this core, or a blocker (named in the message);
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] (read-only mode or connection) and
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] (a Viewer through a hub) with nothing sent;
     * [`DspiError::Locked`] when another session holds the device lock, or
     * this console holds it through `device_lock_acquire`;
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureStart(setup: MeasureSetup): MeasureState

    /**
     * Push one block of the microphone's capture: mono samples, full scale
     * ±1, starting at frame `first_frame` of the client's own count, at
     * `rate_hz`, `clipped` when the converter clipped. The safety checks run
     * first, O(block) (S4, S5): a gap or repeat in the frame indices, a
     * clip, the SPL cap. No analysis runs here. A push with no session, or
     * while the session wants none, is ignored.
     *
     * Errors: none for the capture's content (a fault ends the session and
     * shows in the state); [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for more than
     * 1 Mi samples in one block.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureCapturePush(firstFrame: Long, samples: List<Float>, rateHz: Long, clipped: Boolean): MeasureState

    /**
     * Drive the session: apply queued notifications, judge them (S5), renew
     * the lease, poll the generator and the meters (about 4 Hz), start the
     * next step, run one analysis slice. Call it about every 50 ms while a
     * session is active, off the UI thread (it blocks for a few transfers).
     * Returns the state; never fails.
     */
    fun measureTick(): MeasureState

    /**
     * The microphone is at the next position (or the headphones reseated):
     * go on with the next run.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when the session is not waiting
     * for a position.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureNextPosition(): MeasureState

    /**
     * The user's Stop: `SIGGEN_CTL_STOP_NOW` where the device is reachable,
     * the run discarded, the lock given back. Idempotent; never fails.
     * It ends the live view too (`measure_live_stop`).
     */
    fun measureStop(): MeasureState

    /**
     * The session's state, without I/O.
     */
    fun measureState(): MeasureState

    /**
     * The finished measurement as a `measurement` document's text (schema 1,
     * derived curves only). File it with `measure_doc_put` (or the hub's).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] while no result is ready.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureResult(): String

    /**
     * Forget a finished session (its result included), so the screens show
     * none. Refused while one is active.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] while a session is active.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureClear()

    /**
     * Start the live view of one output (PLAN-P5 T9, T5): short sweeps one
     * after the other, or finite pink noise with the microphone RTA, each
     * run under every rule of a measurement (the preflight, the ramp, the
     * aborts; `measure_start`'s errors). The device lock is held until
     * `measure_live_stop`. Then call `measure_live_tick` about every 50 ms
     * (instead of `measure_tick`) and push capture whenever the session
     * wants it (`measure_capture_push`); read `measure_live_frame`.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a bad setup, a measurement
     * or a live view already running, or a blocker (named);
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Denied`], [`DspiError::Locked`],
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] as `measure_start`, with nothing sent.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureLiveStart(setup: MeasureLiveSetup): MeasureLiveState

    /**
     * Drive the live view: renew the lock's lease, drive the run in flight
     * (`measure_tick`), take a finished sweep's curve, feed the RTA, start
     * the next run. Call it about every 50 ms while the live view runs, off
     * the UI thread. A run's abort ends the live view (its reason in
     * `issue`). Never fails.
     */
    fun measureLiveTick(): MeasureLiveState

    /**
     * What the live view shows now: the measured curve, the target levelled
     * to it, the predicted curve of a pending (or unmeasured) change, the
     * RTA, the pending proposal, the run's state. No I/O; safe from a
     * listener.
     */
    fun measureLiveFrame(): MeasureLiveFrame

    /**
     * Where the live view stands. No I/O; safe from a listener.
     */
    fun measureLiveState(): MeasureLiveState

    /**
     * Stop the live view: the run in flight stopped (one `STOP_NOW`), the
     * lock given back. `measure_stop` does the same. Idempotent; never
     * fails.
     */
    fun measureLiveStop(): MeasureLiveState

    /**
     * Preview `bands` (PEQ bands of `output`, the live view's) as a pending
     * change: the predicted curve shows at once, nothing is written until
     * `measure_live_apply`. An empty list drops the pending change.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when the live view does not
     * measure `output`, a band index is past the output's PEQ, a value is not
     * finite, or a type is not one the curves model; the limits profile's
     * refusal ([`DspiError::LimitExceeded`] and kin) for a write it would
     * refuse.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureLivePredict(output: Int, bands: List<MeasureLiveBand>): MeasureLiveProposal

    /**
     * "Cut this peak": one free PEQ band (flat or bypassed now) from the fit
     * on one octave about `freq_hz`, cut only, previewed as the pending
     * change. `issue` says why nothing is proposed (no curve yet, nothing to
     * cut, not trusted there, no free band).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when the live view does not
     * measure `output`; the limits profile's refusal of the band.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureLiveCutPeak(output: Int, freqHz: Double): MeasureLiveProposal

    /**
     * "Fit this range": the free PEQ bands fitted toward the target in
     * `f1_hz..f2_hz` (held to the measurement's trusted range), with the
     * placement's bounds (S9: boost +3 dB speakers, +6 dB headphones, never
     * above the limits profile's cap; cut -15 dB; Q 0.3 to 10) and a
     * headroom cut of the output gain for its net boost; previewed as the
     * pending change. Every band the fit lists is written on apply.
     *
     * Errors: as [`DspiCore::measure_live_cut_peak`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureLiveFitRange(output: Int, f1Hz: Double, f2Hz: Double): MeasureLiveProposal

    /**
     * Write the pending change `proposal_id` as **one undo group**
     * (unguarded, as a manual edit; S10): the run in flight is stopped, each
     * band through the typed setter (judged by read-only, the limits and the
     * lock as any write), then the next run starts. Refused, with nothing
     * written, unless the device's bands are still the ones the proposal was
     * computed from. A write that fails puts back the ones before it.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for no such pending proposal or
     * a chain changed since the preview; the setters' errors
     * ([`DspiError::ReadOnly`], [`DspiError::Denied`], the limits'
     * refusals, a transfer failure).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureLiveApply(proposalId: Long): MeasureLiveState

    /**
     * Drop the pending change (nothing is written).
     */
    fun measureLiveDiscard(): MeasureLiveState

    /**
     * Hold (`true`) the RTA's spectrum for comparison, or drop the held
     * copy (`false`).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when no live RTA runs, or there
     * is no spectrum to hold yet.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureLiveHold(on: Boolean): MeasureLiveState

    /**
     * The RTA's averaging preset; the average starts again (the held copy
     * is kept).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when no live RTA runs.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureLiveAveraging(averaging: MeasureRtaAveraging): MeasureLiveState

    /**
     * Keep measurement, microphone and target documents in files under
     * `dir`, the client's own data folder (created if missing): a folder per
     * device serial for measurements, `mic/` and `target/`, each with an
     * index. "" forgets the folder.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Io`] when the folder cannot be created;
     * [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on the web (no files there).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureSetDir(dir: String)

    /**
     * The filed documents of `kind` (`measurement`, `mic`, `target`; ""
     * every kind): by kind, then oldest first. Measurements of device
     * `serial` only ("" every device's). No folder named (and the web) is
     * an empty list; a damaged index is rebuilt from the documents and a
     * damaged document set aside (the corrupt-file rule).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an unknown kind.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureDocs(kind: String, serial: String): List<MeasureDocInfo>

    /**
     * Filed document `id`'s text.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an unknown id, one two
     * folders hold, or a damaged document (set aside); [`DspiError::Io`]
     * with no folder named; [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on the web.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureDocGet(id: String): String

    /**
     * File a measurement, microphone or target document (validated by its
     * kind's reader) as a new document; a filed document is never
     * overwritten. Its own id is kept when free, else one is made from its
     * time. A measurement that names no device takes the open device's
     * serial.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a text that is not a valid
     * document of the family (a preset or a snapshot among them), or a
     * measurement with no serial and no device open; [`DspiError::Io`] with
     * no folder named, a failed write, or a full store (100 measurements a
     * device, 32 microphones, 64 targets, 64 MiB);
     * [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on the web.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureDocPut(text: String): MeasureDocInfo

    /**
     * Rename filed document `id`; any Unicode, cut to 256 characters.
     * Errors as [`DspiCore::measure_doc_get`], and [`DspiError::Io`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureDocRename(id: String, name: String): MeasureDocInfo

    /**
     * Set filed document `id`'s note; any Unicode, cut to 4096 characters.
     * Errors as [`DspiCore::measure_doc_rename`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureDocNote(id: String, note: String): MeasureDocInfo

    /**
     * Delete filed document `id`. Errors as [`DspiCore::measure_doc_get`].
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureDocDelete(id: String)

    /**
     * Output `output`'s measured response as REW text ("Import frequency
     * response"): `doc` is a filed measurement's id or a measurement
     * document's text (the web's way). Frequency, dB and the wrapped phase
     * where measured; a header with the name, the output and the
     * microphone. Derived curves only (S12).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an unknown id, a document
     * that is not a measurement, or an output it does not hold; as
     * [`DspiCore::measure_doc_get`] for an id.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measurementExportRew(doc: String, output: Int): String

    /**
     * Turn the demo's simulated microphone on (`true`, its world drawn from
     * `seed`) or off. On, it answers the demo DSPi's generator through
     * P5SIM's three-way speaker in a room and feeds the measurement session
     * from `measure_tick`: the client pushes no capture (its pushes are
     * ignored) and labels the microphone "Simulated microphone". Off, the
     * client's own capture is the microphone again.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] to turn it on over anything but a
     * demo connection (a DSPi on USB, MIDI or through a hub) or in a build
     * without it; [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with nothing open;
     * [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] while a measurement is running.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun measureSimSet(on: Boolean, seed: Long): MeasureSimInfo

    /**
     * The simulated microphone's state, without I/O.
     */
    fun measureSimInfo(): MeasureSimInfo

    /**
     * The mode to show on a client of `platform` (`phone`, `desktop` or
     * `web`) when the user has chosen `chosen` (a mode id, or "" for none):
     * the locked-user state first, then a mode the hub pins, then the
     * user's choice, then the platform's default. With no PIN set nothing
     * asks for one. Never writes; no I/O beyond the PIN's files, read once.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a platform class or a mode
     * the manifest does not have.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun modeResolve(platform: String, chosen: String): ModeChoice

    /**
     * Pin the open device's mode, as the hub says for this console's role
     * (P1HL's `mode.pinned`): a mode id or `locked_user`; "" clears it. It
     * overrides the user's choice until cleared or another device opens.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device;
     * [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an id the manifest does not have.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun modeSetPinned(mode: String)

    /**
     * The mode the hub pins for the open device ("" for none).
     */
    fun modePinned(): String

    /**
     * The monitor lines with an id above `after_id`, oldest first, and the
     * id to pass next time (`after_id` itself when there is nothing new).
     * Pass 0 for everything still kept. Never fails.
     */
    fun monitorRead(afterId: Long): MonitorBatch

    /**
     * Forget every monitor line. Ids carry on from where they were, so a
     * reader's last id stays valid. Never fails.
     */
    fun monitorClear()

    /**
     * Register (`Some`) or clear (`None`) the wake-up listener of the
     * notification reader. It is called on the reader thread whenever a
     * packet was queued (and once when the stream ends), and must only
     * schedule a call to [`DspiCore::process_notifications`].
     *
     * Setting or clearing waits for a call in progress on another thread,
     * so after `None` returns the old listener is not running. Never call
     * this from inside the listener itself (it would wait for itself).
     */
    fun setNotifyListener(listener: NotifyListener?)

    /**
     * Apply every queued notification to the mirror and say what changed:
     * `flags` are the `NOTIFY_*` bits (`crate::consts`), `filter_channels`
     * a bit per wire channel whose PEQ or crossover bands changed.
     *
     * - A parameter another host (a knob, the OS volume, UART, MIDI, ...)
     * changed is patched into the mirror: `NOTIFY_STATE`, plus
     * `NOTIFY_CURVES` when it moves the graph.
     * - Our own echoes are applied silently, unless the device's value
     * differs from the mirror's (then they are reported like a foreign
     * change).
     * - A foreign bulk write, a preset load, lost packets or a change the
     * mirror cannot patch re-read the whole image and the preset
     * directory: `NOTIFY_STATE | NOTIFY_CURVES | NOTIFY_REFRESHED`. A
     * preset load or factory reset also makes the new state the
     * baseline of `preset_dirty`, except what was changed on the device
     * after the load (an edit made before the notification arrived):
     * that stays unsaved. If the re-read fails, the flags say
     * only what was patched, and it is tried again: up to 5 times over
     * about 31 s, each try announced by a call of the [`NotifyListener`].
     * After the last one the result carries [`NOTIFY_STALE`], once; the
     * next change that needs a re-read starts over.
     * - `NOTIFY_DISCONNECTED`: the stream ended because the device went
     * away (never after an intentional close). It is reported on every
     * call until the connection is closed; the caller should close it
     * (`disconnect`, or C1b's hotplug handling). A lost DSPi Link session
     * does not count while the core is reconnecting (`link_status`):
     * leave the connection alone, and the mirror stays last known.
     *
     * Every packet is also recorded in the monitor log
     * ([`DspiCore::monitor_read`]).
     *
     * Without a device, or with nothing queued, it returns an empty
     * result. Never fails and never panics on device data. Blocks only
     * for a re-read (one bulk read and the directory read, plus the input
     * source names after a lost packet).
     */
    fun processNotifications(): NotifyResult

    /**
     * Open the `.dspipreset` document `text` with no device, as the demo
     * device seeded from it (PRO1 W7): the platform and channel counts its
     * `meta` names (else the channels it lists), every setting it holds read
     * as an import reads them, the rest the demo's. `file_name` is what the
     * client shows ("File: <name>"); `meta.name`, else the file's name, names
     * the running preset and the device. Replaces any open device; nothing
     * touches USB. The settings are read (`fetch_all`) and are the baseline
     * for unsaved changes; the undo history starts empty.
     *
     * Returns JSON `{ok: true, fileName, name, kind, platform, inputs,
     * outputs, notes}`, `notes` what reading the document reported (values
     * left alone or clamped, channels this shape lacks).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for text that is not a document
     * this build reads (the reason as a sentence) or one naming no inputs or
     * outputs; [`DspiError::Transfer`] if the device could not be laid out
     * or read. On an error nothing is open.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun openDocument(text: String, fileName: String): String

    /**
     * Whether the open connection is a document ([`DspiCore::open_document`]),
     * as JSON: `{offline: true, fileName, name, kind, platform}`, or
     * `{offline: false}` for a device or none. No I/O.
     */
    fun offlineDocument(): String

    /**
     * The open document as it now stands, a `.dspipreset` document of the
     * kind it was opened as, under its name (`config_export`'s rules;
     * `links` as there). The edits are then the baseline: nothing is unsaved.
     * The client writes the text to the file.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when the connection is not a
     * document (export the configuration instead); otherwise
     * `config_export`'s.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun saveDocument(appVersion: String, links: Int): String

    /**
     * The open device or document as a template: a `.dspipreset` document
     * with `meta.kind = "template"` named `name` (`config_export`'s rules;
     * `links` as there). The client writes it to the user's template
     * folder, where [`template_list`] finds it. Errors as `config_export`'s.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun saveAsTemplate(name: String, appVersion: String, links: Int): String

    /**
     * Every output's label from the mirror, in output order: the user's name,
     * else "Out N · channel · band" from the routing and the filters. Call it
     * when the state changes, not per frame. Never fails.
     */
    fun outputLabels(): List<OutputLabel>

    /**
     * The dashboard's output cards from the mirror: paired by the crossfeed
     * pair map when the device has one, else by slot. Never fails.
     */
    fun outputCards(): List<OutputCard>

    /**
     * PEQ band 0..9 of wire channel `ch`, sent as one whole band
     * (`REQ_SET_EQ_PARAM`).
     *
     * The gain is quantized to 0.1 dB, except on a Linkwitz Transform, where
     * it carries the target frequency fp in Hz. A Linkwitz Transform's
     * target Q `qp` is clamped to 0.1..20; other types ignore `qp` (the
     * mirror reads it back as 0.707).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a channel or band out of
     * range, a non-finite frequency, Q or gain, or a NaN `qp` on a
     * Linkwitz Transform; the connection and transfer errors of every
     * setter ([`DspiError::Disconnected`], [`DspiError::ReadOnly`],
     * [`DspiError::Transfer`]).
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setFilter(ch: Int, band: Int, params: FilterParams)

    /**
     * Crossover band 0..3 of an output's wire channel `ch` (wire band
     * `20 + xband`). The type must be flat (0) or a crossover type (32..63);
     * the gain is sent as given.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an input channel, a band
     * out of range, another filter type or a non-finite value; the
     * connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setCrossover(ch: Int, xband: Int, params: FilterParams)

    /**
     * Bypass (`true`) or re-enable one band of wire channel `ch`, keeping
     * its settings (`REQ_SET_BAND_BYPASS`). `band`: PEQ 0..9, or crossover
     * 20..23 on an output channel.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a channel or band out of
     * range; the connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setBandBypass(ch: Int, band: Int, bypass: Boolean)

    /**
     * Legacy preamp: every input to the same value (`REQ_SET_PREAMP`), in
     * dB, clamped to −60..[`DspiCore::preamp_max_db`] and quantized to
     * 0.1 dB.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a NaN; the connection and
     * transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setPreamp(db: Float)

    /**
     * Preamp of one input (`REQ_SET_PREAMP_CH`), in dB, clamped to
     * −60..[`DspiCore::preamp_max_db`] and quantized to 0.1 dB. Input 0 is
     * also the legacy `preamp_db`.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an input out of range or a
     * NaN; the connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setInputPreamp(input: Int, db: Float)

    /**
     * Preamp of a linked input pair: `input` and its partner (`input ^ 1`,
     * inputs 2p and 2p+1) both to `db`, shaped as
     * [`DspiCore::set_input_preamp`] shapes it, in one undo group
     * ([`DspiCore::begin_group`]): one undo step labelled "Input trim,
     * linked pair". `input`, the side the user moved, is written first.
     * The link itself is the client's (a document's `inputPairLinked`):
     * call this while the pair is linked, `set_input_preamp` otherwise.
     *
     * Both inputs and the value are checked before anything is sent. A
     * failure on the second write leaves the first one written (one undo
     * step takes it back).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an input or its partner
     * out of range, or a NaN; the connection and transfer errors of every
     * setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setInputPairPreamp(input: Int, db: Float)

    /**
     * PEQ band `band` (0..9) of a linked input pair: `input` and its
     * partner (`input ^ 1`, inputs 2p and 2p+1, which are wire channels
     * 2p and 2p+1) both to `params`, as [`DspiCore::set_filter`] writes
     * it, in one undo group ([`DspiCore::begin_group`]): one undo step
     * labelled "EQ band, linked pair", and undo and redo move both sides
     * together. `input`, the side the person edited, is written first.
     * The link itself is the client's (a document's `inputPairLinked`):
     * call this for every band edit while the pair is linked (the band
     * list, a menu, a typed value, each tick of a graph drag), and
     * `set_filter` otherwise.
     *
     * In a drag ([`DspiCore::set_dragging`]) the group joins the drag's
     * step, so a whole drag of a pair's bands is one step that holds each
     * band of each side once (WP P0W2) and undo puts both sides back as
     * they were when the drag began. A value someone else changes on one
     * side is judged on its own, as in every step: undo leaves theirs and
     * names them, and puts the rest back.
     *
     * The inputs, the band and the values are checked before anything is
     * sent. A failure on the second write leaves the first one written
     * (one undo step takes it back).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an input or its partner
     * out of range, a band out of range, or the values `set_filter`
     * refuses; the connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setInputPairFilter(input: Int, band: Int, params: FilterParams)

    /**
     * Bypass (`true`) or re-enable PEQ band `band` (0..9) of a linked
     * input pair, both sides, keeping their settings: `input` and then
     * `input ^ 1` through [`DspiCore::set_band_bypass`], in one undo group
     * with the label of [`DspiCore::set_input_pair_filter`]. One undo
     * step; checks and errors as there.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setInputPairBandBypass(input: Int, band: Int, bypass: Boolean)

    /**
     * Bypass the whole EQ (`true`) or run it.
     *
     * Errors: the connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setBypass(enabled: Boolean)

    /**
     * Device master volume in dB: −128 (or anything below) mutes, otherwise
     * −127..0 in 0.5 dB steps (rounded, then clamped).
     *
     * The firmware answers with the legacy v1 volume notification and then
     * a `PARAM_CHANGED`; both are the echo of this write.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a NaN; the connection and
     * transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setMasterVolume(db: Float)

    /**
     * 0 = master volume independent of presets, 1 = saved with presets.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for another mode; the
     * connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setMasterVolumeMode(mode: Int)

    /**
     * FLASH WRITE: store the live master volume for the independent mode
     * (`REQ_SAVE_MASTER_VOLUME`), under the device lock like the other
     * flash writes. Changes nothing in the mirror.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Refused`] with the device's status byte
     * (`PRESET_*`) when the save fails; [`DspiError::Locked`] /
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] (Link); the connection and transfer errors of
     * every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun saveMasterVolume()

    /**
     * User volume (what the host's volume slider drives) in dB, clamped to
     * −60..0.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a NaN; the connection and
     * transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setUserVolume(db: Float)

    /**
     * Mute (`true`) or unmute the user volume.
     *
     * Errors: the connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setUserMute(muted: Boolean)

    /**
     * Delay of wire channel `ch` in ms, quantized to 0.0001 ms; below 0 it
     * is 0. An output channel's delay is its output delay, so it goes
     * through [`DspiCore::set_output_delay`].
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a channel out of range, a
     * NaN, or a delay above the platform's [`DspiCore::max_delay_ms`] (the
     * message names it; nothing is sent); the connection and transfer
     * errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setDelay(ch: Int, ms: Float)

    /**
     * Loudness compensation on (`true`) or off.
     *
     * Errors: the connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setLoudness(enabled: Boolean)

    /**
     * Loudness reference level in dB SPL, clamped to 40..100 as the
     * firmware does.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a NaN; the connection and
     * transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setLoudnessRef(spl: Float)

    /**
     * Loudness intensity in percent, clamped to 0..200 as the firmware does.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a NaN; the connection and
     * transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setLoudnessIntensity(pct: Float)

    /**
     * Bit k: loudness compensates output k.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on firmware without the per-output
     * mask; the connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setLoudnessMask(mask: Int)

    /**
     * Crossfeed on (`true`) or off.
     *
     * Errors: the connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setCrossfeed(enabled: Boolean)

    /**
     * Crossfeed preset: 0 default, 1 Chu Moy, 2 Meier, 3 custom. Only the
     * preset is sent: the device keeps its custom frequency and feed whatever
     * the preset. While a named preset (0 to 2) is chosen, `get_state` gives
     * that preset's frequency and feed (700 Hz / 4.5 dB, 700 Hz / 6 dB,
     * 650 Hz / 9.5 dB), which the device runs; with Custom (3) it gives the
     * custom pair again. A preset export, the bulk image and undo keep the
     * custom pair.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for another preset; the
     * connection and transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setCrossfeedPreset(preset: Int)

    /**
     * Custom crossfeed frequency in Hz, clamped to 500..2000 as the firmware
     * does.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a NaN; the connection and
     * transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setCrossfeedFreq(freq: Float)

    /**
     * Custom crossfeed feed level in dB, clamped to 0..15 as the firmware
     * does.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a NaN; the connection and
     * transfer errors of every setter.
     */
    @Throws(DspiException::class)
    fun setCrossfeedFeed(feed: Float)

    /**
     * The crossfeed's custom pair (frequency and feed) as the device keeps
